// main.mn — the executable manifest (per the entry-handlers walkthrough §1.4
// + the CLI vocabulary walkthrough). One file. One function. The `~>`
// chain IS the manifest. What the program has access to is visible
// on the page. No JSON config. No TOML lockfile. The type system
// IS the package manager.
//
// IC.4: compile / check / etc take entry-MODULE names (e.g.
// "compiler/main") instead of raw sources. The driver walks the
// import DAG in dependency layers and infers each module; cross-run
// module skip is the §4④ image-persist peer
// (Hβ.persist.module-image-cache), never a side-file serializer.
//
// CLI canonical vocabulary (per the CLI vocabulary walkthrough):
//
//   mentl                       → bare: the directory PROJECTION on a tty;
//                                 a pipe/file on fd 0 compiles stdin
//   mentl edit [path] [--port=N] → canonical Web IDE (browser-WASM holographic)
//   mentl doc [path]             → documentation projection (F.1 substrate)
//   mentl serve                  → LSP transport on stdio
//   mentl compile <path>         → emit WAT to stdout
//   mentl run <path>             → compile; the shim executes (WASI has no exec)
//   mentl check <path>           → diagnostics; no emit
//   mentl test [path]            → test handler chain
//   mentl audit <path>           → capability set + severance suggestions
//   mentl query <path> <q>       → structured ad-hoc query
//   mentl teach [path]           → terminal voice surface; gradient narration
//   mentl repl                   → line REPL over current env
//   mentl new <name>             → write <name>.mn here (one file; imports are the manifest)
//   mentl --with <handler>       → universal escape hatch; resolves by env_lookup
//
// Drift 38 (mascot-as-tentacle-prefix per CLAUDE.md red-flag table):
// project-action verbs are canonical (the binary IS named Mentl;
// subcommand prefix is structural like `git commit` / `cargo build`).
// Tentacle-named verbs (Trace / Why / Propose / Unlock / Verify /
// Topology) that shadow at-cursor projections would be drift 38 —
// see tools/drift-patterns.tsv mode 38 + the CLI vocabulary walkthrough
// §3.3 for the line. `teach` and `query` are project-action peer
// transports (canonical) for terminal users — those tentacle names
// have legitimate CLI semantics that the per-cursor projection
// inside the medium does not duplicate.
//
// Module names map to paths: `main` → `src/main.mn`. Strip `src/` or
// `lib/` prefixes and `.mn` suffix from a path to get the module name.
//
// The outermost handlers — diagnostics, wat_stdout, wasi_filesystem,
// terminal_transport — are the sandbox boundary. Every inward
// handler receives narrower capability.
import pipeline

import asm

import backends/wasm

import board

import cli

import cursor

import cursor_transport

import driver

import format

import fs

import infer

import io

import lsp

import mcp

import own

import query

import rename

import sections

import space

import synth_proposer

import test

// The verb grammar — EntryVerb, the VerbSpec table, parse_cli_args and
// its address/target helpers — lives in src/cli.mn now (PLAN §11 Phase
// 3.5, the mcp→main seam: one grammar serves two transports, so it
// lives where both can import it). This module keeps the DISPATCH:
// running the proven verb is the entry's own job.
//
// ─── Entry — argv → Invocation → dispatch ─────────────────────────
//
// Per EH §1.4: argv parses into EntryHandlerInvocation; dispatch
// resolves the named entry-handler and runs it. Per Insight #1
// (handler chain IS capability stack): each entry-handler's chain
// places the surface adapter (terminal_transport / lsp_adapter /
// wat_stdout) OUTERMOST = least trusted = sandbox boundary.
// The PROCESS intern table — the root absorb for the stray Intern
// extents (discovery parses, solo renders, session wire reads) that run
// outside any infer_context bracket. Inner brackets install their own
// tables and SHADOW this one for their whole extent, so a judgment's
// intern identity never mixes with a stray's (a stray's handles stay
// inside its own extent by construction). Intern is evidence-tier (two
// handlers), so the executable gate's strict conjunct clears only
// row-wise — this install IS the row cure and the live chain node the
// stray performs dispatch through. Like every table, it is born holding
// the medium's own vocabulary (intern.mn's state init).
// A failure no route answers nearer is the process's: `fail_said` says it on
// stderr and exits 1. Every route's filesystem install performs `Fail` where
// an op cannot do what it was asked, and its arms resolve outside the
// install, so a path that does not read stops the verb naming the path.
// Outermost, stderr has one writer: a live line where fd 2 is a terminal,
// the full lines alone into a pipe (`progress_stderr`).
fn main(argv) = match parse_cli_args(argv) {
  ParseError(msg) => print_error_and_help(msg),
  Invocation(verb) => dispatch_invocation(verb),
} ~> diagnostics_handler ~> intern_table ~> fail_said ~> progress_stderr(fd_is_tty(2))

// dispatch_invocation — the substrate handlers (env/graph/diagnostics)
// install ONCE at this outermost dispatch boundary: the program boundary
// IS an install site (EH). Every verb path — including import-parse,
// which runs BEFORE compile_source's per-compile chain — performs
// against a rooted handler stack; the evidence chain from main downward
// forwards real entries, never a bare closure record. Inner per-compile
// installs shadow these (innermost wins), giving each compile_source
// fresh graph state.
//
// NO schedule handler installs here: the wheel's own algorithm carries
// zero fanouts (one sequential compile spine — §5.O layer 4 is the
// multi-core `><` that will bring its install WITH it), and a standing
// parallel_compose install would put the spawn arm's task-record
// performs into the wheel's own emitted tree, flipping the wheel to the
// shared-image/shared-cell shape for parallelism it never performs. A
// schedule installs lexically at the fanout it schedules — SYNTAX §`><`:
// no Schedule installed → Seq, the invisible default.
fn dispatch_invocation(verb) = match verb {
  VEdit(target) => edit_run(target),
  VDoc(target) => doc_run(target),
  VMap(name) => map_run(name),
  VServe => serve_run(),
  VSpace(addr) => space_run(addr),
  VTighten(target) => tighten_run(target),
  VFmt(mode, targets) => fmt_run(mode, targets),
  VCompile(target) => compile_run(target),
  VAsm(source) => asm_run(source),
  VResume(img) => resume_run(img),
  // The bare invocation forks on the ONE substrate fact that
  // distinguishes intent: fd 0's filetype. A pipe or file IS the
  // compile-stdin contract (`mentl < wheel.mn` — the fixpoint path,
  // untouched); a terminal is a human with no program yet — project
  // the welcome instead of hanging on a read that never comes.
  VCompileStdin => if fd_is_tty(0) { welcome_run() } else { compile_stdin_run() },
  VRun(target, _) => run_run(target),
  VLand => land_run(),
  VCheck(target) => check_run(target),
  VAt(target, line, col) => at_run(target, line, col),
  VAccept(target, line, col) => accept_run(target, line, col),
  VRename(target, line, col, name) => rename_run(target, line, col, name),
  VTest(target) => test_run(target),
  VAudit(target) => audit_run(target),
  VQuery(target, q) => query_run(target, q),
  VTeach(target) => teach_run(target),
  VRepl => repl_run(),
  VMarch => march_run(),
  VVerify(target) => verify_run(target),
  VMcp => mcp_run(session_at_read),
  VSession => session_run(session_answer),
  VNew(name) => new_project(name),
  VWith(hname, target) => with_run(hname, target),
  VWelcome => welcome_run(),
} ~> judgment_ledgers ~> env_handler(env_root()) ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version()) ~> diagnostics_handler

// ─── edit_run — the cursor session at the terminal
//
// Each step reads the gradient's argmax, projects the eight-aspect
// CursorView, surfaces it through terminal_transport, receives the
// person's action and applies it (`patch_to_file` writes the accepted
// patch). Silence only when nothing is left to propose — the gradient at
// its top.
fn edit_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    // default project entry
    None => "main",
  }
  // The session's final TransportState is a record pointer, not an exit
  // code (main's result IS the process exit); the chain is a statement —
  // its value drops — and a completed session exits 0. The whole analysis
  // core installs as one bracket (infer_context); cursor_default is the
  // innermost projection handler, its arms performing Synth, Teach, Verify
  // and Graph ops that reach the installs around them. The session draws its
  // own screen, so the compile's steps are never drawn over it.
  mentl_edit_session(entry_module) ~> patch_to_file ~> terminal_transport ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default ~> wasi_filesystem ~> infer_context ~> progress_stderr(false)
  0
}

// The session reads the link the address transport projects (`driver_link`),
// and the caret OPENS AT THE MODULE IT NAMES — its own node, line 1 of its
// own file — so the module-aware proximity ranks that module's absences above
// the rest of the link's.
fn mentl_edit_session(entry_module) = match driver_link(entry_module) {
  None => eprint_string("mentl edit: {entry_module} did not load — nothing to edit\n"),
  Some(mh) => {
    patch_set_target(entry_module)
    cursor_session(entry_start_caret(mh))
    ()
  },
}

// The entry's opening caret stands at the entry's MODULE NODE — line 1 of
// its own file, and the module every position the session ranks belongs
// to. A caret at the link would name no module, so the session would rank
// the whole link and open on the prelude.
fn entry_start_caret(mh) =
  Caret(mh, Located(Span(1, 1, 1, 1), Inferred("mentl edit session start — at the entry")))

// ─── space_run — the ide is the page (`mentl space`), and the View at an
// address (`mentl space <file>:<line>[:<col>]`)
//
// Mentl Space is static files: the page, the boot and the modules
// ide/space.manifest names, served by any host that sets the two
// cross-origin-isolation headers, or by the page's own service worker
// where a host sets none. The wheel owns no listener — WASI p1 has no
// bind/listen, and the medium carries no socket seam past it — so the bare
// verb reached here says where the page is served from: the mentl command
// stages the manifest and serves the staged directory.
//
// At an address the verb answers the View the page paints — the ring at
// the caret, the Lens and the Ledger of its module — as one JSON value on
// stdout (`space_project`), through the chain the address form runs
// (`at_run`) with the diagnostic bank installed, so a judgment's reports
// reach the Lens as the kinds they are and its tightenings reach the Ledger.
// The resident session answers the same verb over the graph it keeps
// (`session_read`).
fn space_run(addr) = match addr {
  None => {
    eprint_string("mentl space: the page is served by the mentl command — from the repo, `bash tools/space-stage.sh && python3 tools/space-serve.py`, then open http://127.0.0.1:7397/ (the deployed page is https://space.ampactor.dev/); `mentl space <file>:<line>[:<col>]` answers the View at an address\n")
    2
  },
  Some(SpaceAt(target, line, col, needle)) => space_project(driver_link(target), target, line, col, needle) ~> patch_to_file ~> terminal_transport ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> infer_context,
}

// ─── march_run — the medium judges its own generation (`mentl march`)
//
// The wheel is src/main.mn's import DAG plus the prelude edge — the one
// link model every compile reads (driver.mn), judged cold and in-process,
// with the emission STREAMED through wat_to_file — bytes reach the host at
// every segment, so the module's text is never held whole (the collecting
// sink's scope lives at wat_to_string's decl). The verb names the modules
// its link judged (`march: link …`), which is what tools/march.sh holds
// against `mentl query src/main.mn modules`: one link model, read twice,
// must answer one set. Whether a generation reproduces itself needs the
// host to run the generation this verb just emitted — a compiled module is
// run by the mentl command, never by the compiler that reached it — so the
// comparison of generations is tools/march.sh's, and the verb spends
// nothing on one.
fn march_run() = match march_entry() {
  None => {
    print_string("march: no wheel source found — run beside the repo (src/main.mn) or through the installed shim\n")
    1
  },
  Some(entry) => {
    let wat_path = ".build/march/m2.wat"
    let new_path = ".build/march/m2.new"
    fs_mkdir(".build")
    fs_mkdir(".build/march")
    let fd = fs_create(new_path)
    match march_emit(entry, fd) {
      (names, errors) => {
        fs_close(fd)
        print_string("march: link {join(" ", names)}\n")
        if errors > 0 {
          fs_unlink(new_path)
          print_string("march: {int_to_str(len(names))} modules · census {int_to_str(errors)}\n")
          print_string("march: the judgment REFUSES — no verdict from a refused wheel\n")
          1
        } else {
          fs_rename(new_path, wat_path)
          print_string("march: {int_to_str(len(names))} modules · census 0 · the generation is {wat_path}\n")
          0
        }
      },
    }
  },
} ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version())

// The link's judgment with the streaming sink, as `mentl compile` reads it
// (cold: the fixed point never restores an image) and under ScopeAll, so the
// census is the whole link's. The census count and the modules' names are
// read INSIDE the install that holds the graph (the count and the counter
// are one handler — pipeline_check's own law), and only those values cross
// out of it.
fn march_emit(entry, fd) = {
  driver_link_scoped(entry, Census)
  compile_remainder()
  (link_modules() |> map({ mh => module_of(mh).path }), diag_errors())
} ~> wat_to_file(fd) ~> wasi_filesystem ~> compile_context

// The wheel's entry: the repo's own main, or the installed shim's mount when
// the verb runs outside the repo. The repo's is named as the CLI names it
// (`main`, path_to_module's spelling), so the verb's link and
// `mentl compile src/main.mn`'s are one link with one set of module names.
fn march_entry() = if fs_exists("src/main.mn") {
  Some("main")
} else if fs_exists("/mentl-home/src/main.mn") {
  Some("/mentl-home/src/main")
} else {
  None
}

// ─── fmt_run — the canonical render written back (`mentl fmt`)
//
// Layout is projection, never contract (SYNTAX principle 1): the file
// re-renders from the graph in canonical form. The render is TOTAL over
// the surface (every Expr/Stmt/Pat variant has its arm — the formatter
// arc's totality landing), carries the comment weave, and re-escapes
// string content, so format∘parse is the identity the module's own
// comment always claimed. Idempotence is the gate: the second render is
// byte-identical to the first.
//
// THE PROJECT is the unit (cargo-style, and stronger): no path renders every
// .mn under the working directory (`mn_files`), a directory its tree, a file
// itself. THE RENDER IS A PARSE: layout is a projection of the parse, and
// this module's formatter imports no inference, so the verb lexes, parses
// and renders — it judges nothing, and so narrates nothing but each file's
// own parse. (It used to judge the entry's whole weave first; the solo route
// before that narrated every dependency's diagnostics at a developer who
// named one file, and the weave was taken to scope them. A parse has nothing
// to scope.) The cost of an operation is the size of its answer (PLAN §5.O),
// and the cost of a file whose bytes the verb last saw canonical is one hash:
// `.build/fmt.stamps` is the medium's memory of its own fixpoint, keyed by
// content under this build's identity. Each file renders in its own arena,
// so a project pass holds one file's scratch at a time.
//
// THE WRITE IS ATOMIC ACROSS THE PROJECT: every file is rendered and proven
// before any is written, and one render that would lose what its author
// wrote writes nothing at all. `--check` writes nothing either, and names
// each file that is not canonical with its first changed line — the answer
// a commit hook and the board read.
fn fmt_run(mode, targets) = {
  let files = fmt_targets(targets)
  let key = "{int_to_str(image_key())}"
  let stamps = fmt_stamps_read(key)
  let verdicts = map(fmt_target_verdict(smap_from_pairs(stamps), ??), files)
  fmt_settle(mode, key, stamps, verdicts)
} ~> wasi_filesystem

// What one target named: a file to judge, or nothing at all.
type FmtTarget = FmtPath(String) | FmtNothing(String)

// A file is what it names; a directory is its tree; a name that is neither
// is a module, read where the resolver files it (`mentl fmt main`). A target
// that names none of the three is refused by name, so the project write
// holds back: a module with no file is never given one.
fn fmt_targets(targets) = if len(targets) == 0 {
  map(FmtPath, mn_files(".", Tree))
} else {
  flat_map(fmt_target_files, targets)
}

fn fmt_target_files(t) = match fs_list_dir(t) {
  Some(_) => map(FmtPath, mn_files(dir_base(t), Tree)),
  None => if fs_exists(t) {
    [FmtPath(t)]
  } else {
    match driver_module_path(path_to_module(t)) {
      Some(path) => [FmtPath(path)],
      None => [FmtNothing(t)],
    }
  },
}

fn fmt_target_verdict(stamps, target) = match target {
  FmtPath(path) => fmt_judge(stamps, path),
  FmtNothing(t) => FmtRefused(
    t,
    [
      "names no directory, no file and no module (searched {join(", ", module_probes(path_to_module(t)))})"
    ]
  ),
}

// One file's verdict, decided before anything is written.
type FmtVerdict
  // the source is its own render: the path and its stamp
  = FmtCanonical(String, String)
  // the path, the source and the canonical render
  | FmtRenders(String, String, String)
  // the path and why it will not be written, one line each
  | FmtRefused(String, [String])
  // the path and the count of its parse's reports no render can fix: a file
  // the parser could not read has no canonical form to compare — a fixture
  // that refuses on purpose, or a file mid-edit — so it is named, never
  // rendered, and holds no other file's write back
  | FmtUnparsed(String, Int)

fn fmt_judge(stamps, path: String) = fmt_file(stamps, path) ~> arena

fn fmt_file(stamps, path: String) = {
  let src = fs_read_file(path)
  let stamp = fmt_stamp(src)
  match smap_get(stamps, path) {
    Some(seen) => if seen == stamp { FmtCanonical(path, stamp) } else { fmt_render_file(path, src) },
    None => fmt_render_file(path, src),
  }
}

// The render. A file the parser refuses is never rendered: the parse is
// productive under error, and a recovered tree written back would canonize
// what the recovery dropped. The verb writes its own FIXPOINT: render once,
// re-parse, render again — a mid-line prose attach moves to its line-start
// home in the first render and is stable from the second, so the WRITTEN
// file is idempotent in one invocation; a render that does not re-parse is
// refused with the parse's own report. Each parse runs inside the file's
// own module (`parse_one_module`), as every compile's does, so a report
// names the file it is about.
fn fmt_render_file(path: String, src: String) = {
  let module = path_to_module(path)
  let from = graph_next()
  let ast = parse_one_module(mint_module(module, path, src, User))
  let to = graph_next()
  let parse_errors = parse_refusals()
  if parse_errors > 0 {
    FmtUnparsed(path, parse_errors)
  } else {
    let first = respell(src, format_program(ast))
    let again = parse_one_module(mint_module(module, path, first, User))
    if parse_refusals() > 0 {
      FmtRefused(path, ["the canonical render does not parse — the formatter's defect, reported above"])
    } else {
      let rendered = respell(first, format_program(again))
      if rendered == src {
        FmtCanonical(path, fmt_stamp(src))
      } else {
        match fmt_losses(src, rendered, lifted_atoms(from, to, ast)) {
          [] => FmtRenders(path, src, rendered),
          lines => FmtRefused(path, lines),
        }
      }
    }
  }
} ~> format_default ~> infer_context

// The parse's reports a render cannot fix: every report but the formatter's
// own lifts. A MachineApplicable class is the lift — a duplicate import is a
// syntax error whose fix IS this render — so it never holds the render back.
// Any other report says the parse recovered from text it could not read, and
// a render of what the recovery built is not what the author wrote, whatever
// the report's severity: an unexpected token narrates, and the holes its
// recovery minted were written back as `??` over the author's clause.
fn parse_refusals() = len(filter(render_cannot_fix, diag_banked()))

fn render_cannot_fix(entry) = match entry {
  (d, _, _, _) => diag_applicability(d) != MachineApplicable,
}

// THE CONSERVATION GATE — the verb lexes its own render beside the source
// and compares comment-token counts: the lexer emits one TComment per
// authored line, so the difference IS the number of prose lines the round
// trip loses (this absorbed the hand-run comment census — ⟳; and the LEXICAL
// count sees lines the parse never attaches, which a weave-to-weave compare
// was blind to). AND THE NAMES: every identifier and literal the source lexes
// to must lex out of the render as well (`lost_atoms_beside`). A render that drops
// one says something else — measured 2026-09-28, when the effect head's
// parameter render deleted `rate: Int`'s annotation, the declared
// `Sample(48000)` then refused, and this gate's sibling reported "prose
// conserved" over the loss. A lossy render is NOT WRITTEN, prose or name:
// the verb names what would be lost and leaves the file as authored.
fn fmt_losses(src: String, rendered: String, stated: [String]) = {
  let src_toks = lex(src)
  let out_toks = lex(rendered)
  let src_lines = count_comment_tokens(src_toks, 0, 0)
  let out_lines = count_comment_tokens(out_toks, 0, 0)
  let lost = lost_atoms_beside(src_toks, out_toks, stated)
  if out_lines >= src_lines && len(lost) == 0 {
    []
  } else {
    [
      "the canonical render ({int_to_str(byte_len(rendered))} bytes beside the source's {int_to_str(byte_len(src))}) loses {int_to_str(src_lines - out_lines)} of {int_to_str(src_lines)} prose line(s) and {int_to_str(len(lost))} authored name(s) or literal(s)"
    ] ++ lost_comment_lines(src_toks, out_toks, 0, []) ++ map(lost_atom_line, take(8, lost))
  }
}

fn lost_atom_line(t) = match token_span(t) {
  Span(line, _, _, _) => match atom_key(t) {
    Some(k) => "lost: {k} (line {int_to_str(line)})",
    None => "lost: a token at line {int_to_str(line)}",
  },
}

// The gate NAMES its losses: each source comment line whose text appears
// nowhere in the render's lex, with its line number — the report that
// retired the external diff for good (up to eight, the shape is what
// matters).
fn lost_comment_lines(ref src_toks, ref out_toks, i, acc) = if i >= len(src_toks) || len(acc) >= 8 {
  acc
} else {
  match token_kind(src_toks[i]) {
    TComment(t) => if comment_text_present(out_toks, t, 0) {
      lost_comment_lines(src_toks, out_toks, i + 1, acc)
    } else {
      lost_comment_lines(src_toks, out_toks, i + 1, push(acc, "lost: //{t}"))
    },
    TFn | TLet | TIf | TElse | TMatch | TType | TEffect | THandler | TWith | TResume | TImport
    | TWhere | TOwn | TRef | TPure | TTrue | TFalse | TIdent(_) | TIntLit(_) | TFloatLit(_)
    | TStringPart(_) | TStringSplice | TEqEq | TBangEq | TLtEq | TGtEq | TArrow | TFatArrow | TPlusPlus
    | TPipeGt | TLtPipe | TGtLt | TTildeGt | TLtTilde | TAndAnd | TOrOr | TLParen | TRParen | TLBrace
    | TRBrace | TLBracket | TRBracket | TComma | TDot | TColon | TSemicolon | TPlus | TMinus | TStar
    | TSlash | TPercent | TEq | TLt | TGt | TBang | TAmp | TPipe | TAt | THole | TNewline | TEof => lost_comment_lines(src_toks, out_toks, i + 1, acc),
  }
}

// The project's verdict. A refusal anywhere writes nothing anywhere: the
// write is the project's, so a file that would lose an atom keeps every
// other file as authored too, and the exit says so. Every file the verb
// judged is named whatever else it found — a refusal beside them never
// hides a file that is not canonical, in either mode.
fn fmt_settle(mode, key: String, stamps, verdicts) = {
  let refused = filter(fmt_is_refused, verdicts)
  let renders = filter(fmt_is_render, verdicts)
  each(fmt_print_unparsed, verdicts)
  each(fmt_print_refused, refused)
  each(fmt_print_would, renders)
  match mode {
    FmtCheck => {
      fmt_stamps_write(key, stamps, verdicts)
      if len(renders) + len(refused) > 0 {
        print_string("fmt: {int_to_str(len(renders))} of {int_to_str(len(verdicts))} file(s) not canonical, {int_to_str(len(refused))} refused — `mentl fmt` writes the first\n")
        1
      } else {
        0
      }
    },
    FmtWrite => if len(refused) > 0 {
      print_string("fmt: nothing written — {int_to_str(len(refused))} file(s) refused\n")
      1
    } else {
      each(fmt_write, renders)
      fmt_stamps_write(key, stamps, map(fmt_written, verdicts))
      0
    },
  }
}

fn fmt_is_refused(v) = match v {
  FmtRefused(_, _) => true,
  FmtCanonical(_, _) | FmtRenders(_, _, _) | FmtUnparsed(_, _) => false,
}

fn fmt_is_render(v) = match v {
  FmtRenders(_, _, _) => true,
  FmtCanonical(_, _) | FmtRefused(_, _) | FmtUnparsed(_, _) => false,
}

fn fmt_print_refused(v) = match v {
  FmtRefused(path, lines) => {
    print_string("fmt: {path} NOT written\n")
    each(fmt_print_reason, lines)
  },
  FmtCanonical(_, _) | FmtRenders(_, _, _) | FmtUnparsed(_, _) => (),
}

fn fmt_print_reason(line) = print_string("  {line}\n")

fn fmt_print_unparsed(v) = match v {
  FmtUnparsed(path, n) => print_string("fmt: {path} not rendered — its parse recovered from {int_to_str(n)} problem(s) a render cannot fix\n"),
  FmtCanonical(_, _) | FmtRenders(_, _, _) | FmtRefused(_, _) => (),
}

// What would change, named at the first line that does — the source's line
// and the render's beside it.
fn fmt_print_would(v) = match v {
  FmtRenders(path, src, rendered) => match first_changed_line(src, rendered) {
    (n, was, now) => print_string("fmt: {path}:{int_to_str(n)} not canonical\n  - {was}\n  + {now}\n"),
  },
  FmtCanonical(_, _) | FmtRefused(_, _) | FmtUnparsed(_, _) => (),
}

fn fmt_write(v) = match v {
  FmtRenders(path, _, rendered) => {
    fs_write_file(path, rendered)
    print_string("fmt: wrote {path}\n")
  },
  FmtCanonical(_, _) | FmtRefused(_, _) | FmtUnparsed(_, _) => (),
}

// After a write, every file the verb saw is canonical at its new bytes.
fn fmt_written(v) = match v {
  FmtRenders(path, _, rendered) => FmtCanonical(path, fmt_stamp(rendered)),
  FmtCanonical(_, _) | FmtRefused(_, _) | FmtUnparsed(_, _) => v,
}

// The first line at which two texts differ, 1-based, with each side's line.
fn first_changed_line(a: String, b: String) = first_changed_from(a, b, 0, 0, 1)

fn first_changed_from(a: String, b: String, i, start, line) = {
  let na = byte_len(a)
  let nb = byte_len(b)
  if i >= na || i >= nb || byte_at(a, i) != byte_at(b, i) {
    (line, line_from(a, start), line_from(b, start))
  } else if byte_at(a, i) == 10 {
    first_changed_from(a, b, i + 1, i + 1, line + 1)
  } else {
    first_changed_from(a, b, i + 1, start, line)
  }
}

fn line_from(s: String, start) = str_slice(s, start, line_end(s, start))

fn line_end(s: String, i) =
  if i >= byte_len(s) || byte_at(s, i) == 10 { i } else { line_end(s, i + 1) }

// ─── .build/fmt.stamps — the medium's memory of its own fixpoint ─────
//
// A stamp is the content's hash beside its length, so a file whose bytes
// changed cannot read as the bytes fmt last saw canonical. The whole file
// carries this build's identity (`image_key`, a hash of the function table,
// the interned data and the global census) on its first line: a different
// formatter is a different build, and a stamp it never wrote is not read.
fn fmt_stamp(src: String) = "{int_to_str(str_hash(src))}:{int_to_str(byte_len(src))}"

fn fmt_stamps_path() = ".build/fmt.stamps"

// The stamps as (path, stamp) pairs, empty when the file is absent or was
// written by another build.
fn fmt_stamps_read(key: String) = if fs_exists(fmt_stamps_path()) {
  match text_lines(fs_read_file(fmt_stamps_path())) {
    [head, ...rest] => if head == key { flat_map(stamp_pair, rest) } else { [] },
    [] => [],
  }
} else {
  []
}

// One line, `<stamp>\t<path>`.
fn stamp_pair(line: String) = match tab_at(line, 0) {
  Some(t) => [(str_slice(line, t + 1, byte_len(line)), str_slice(line, 0, t))],
  None => [],
}

fn tab_at(s: String, i) =
  if i >= byte_len(s) { None } else if byte_at(s, i) == 9 { Some(i) } else { tab_at(s, i + 1) }

fn text_lines(s: String) = text_lines_from(s, 0, [])

fn text_lines_from(s: String, start, acc) = if start >= byte_len(s) {
  acc
} else {
  let e = line_end(s, start)
  text_lines_from(s, e + 1, push(acc, str_slice(s, start, e)))
}

// The verdicts' stamps, and every earlier stamp for a path this run did not
// read: a run over one file keeps what the project's last run learned.
fn fmt_stamps_write(key: String, stamps, verdicts) = {
  let fresh = flat_map(verdict_stamp, verdicts)
  let seen = smap_from_pairs(fresh)
  let kept = filter(stamp_unseen(seen, ??), stamps)
  fs_mkdir(".build")
  fs_write_file(fmt_stamps_path(), join("\n", [key] ++ map(stamp_line, fresh ++ kept)) ++ "\n")
}

fn verdict_stamp(v) = match v {
  FmtCanonical(path, stamp) => [(path, stamp)],
  FmtRenders(_, _, _) | FmtRefused(_, _) | FmtUnparsed(_, _) => [],
}

fn stamp_unseen(seen, pair) = match pair {
  (path, _) => smap_get(seen, path) == None,
}

fn stamp_line(pair) = match pair {
  (path, stamp) => "{stamp}\t{path}",
}

fn smap_from_pairs(pairs) = fold(smap_new(), smap_add_pair, pairs)

fn smap_add_pair(m, pair) = match pair {
  (k, v) => smap_add(m, k, v),
}

fn comment_text_present(ref toks, t: String, i) -> Bool = if i >= len(toks) {
  false
} else {
  match token_kind(toks[i]) {
    TComment(u) => if u == t { true } else { comment_text_present(toks, t, i + 1) },
    TFn | TLet | TIf | TElse | TMatch | TType | TEffect | THandler | TWith | TResume | TImport
    | TWhere | TOwn | TRef | TPure | TTrue | TFalse | TIdent(_) | TIntLit(_) | TFloatLit(_)
    | TStringPart(_) | TStringSplice | TEqEq | TBangEq | TLtEq | TGtEq | TArrow | TFatArrow | TPlusPlus
    | TPipeGt | TLtPipe | TGtLt | TTildeGt | TLtTilde | TAndAnd | TOrOr | TLParen | TRParen | TLBrace
    | TRBrace | TLBracket | TRBracket | TComma | TDot | TColon | TSemicolon | TPlus | TMinus | TStar
    | TSlash | TPercent | TEq | TLt | TGt | TBang | TAmp | TPipe | TAt | THole | TNewline | TEof => comment_text_present(toks, t, i + 1),
  }
}

// ─── tighten_run — the medium authors its own row tightening
//
// The self-authorship driver at its smallest real scope (the self-build
// audit's step 1b): T_OverDeclared already IS a proposal — the diagnostic
// carries the declared row, the proven body row, the fn name, and the
// decl span, MachineApplicable. This verb turns the first authorable one
// into the patch: rewrite the decl's with-clause to the body's proven
// row, report old → new, and leave the verdict to a fresh check + the
// gates. Tightening is the monotone-safe direction — a narrower
// declaration can only shrink what callers accumulate, so it can create
// new T_OverDeclared warnings (the next ratchet turns) but never an
// E_EffectMismatch.
// (The bank the candidates are read from — diag_bank, and tightenables
// over it — lives in pipeline.mn: the analysis layer's own machinery,
// installable by the CLI chains here AND the resident session's bracket in
// mcp.mn; imports flow main → mcp → pipeline, so pipeline is the one
// visible home.)
fn tighten_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  {
    driver_link(entry_module)
    let cands = tightenables()
    let rosters = project_sites(catch_all_refusals())
    // The refuted ownership markers first: a release rewrites one word of a
    // head and moves no line, so the clause patches below read the same line
    // numbers whichever runs first.
    let released = own_releases() |> fold(0, apply_own_release)
    // Batch-apply: every proposal was computed against the CURRENT
    // declarations, and the patch is the clause's RESIDUE — the negations
    // and instance pins the judgment cannot infer (A4, 2026-09-27) — so a
    // batch member never narrows below what the body proves: the positive
    // row it drops is projected, not lost, and nothing authored is ever a
    // row the parser cannot read back. Per file, patch bottom-up so line
    // offsets hold.
    let n = apply_tightenings(cands)
    // The written types the judgment derives, lifted: every function
    // declaration of the project's own layer, the nested among them, asked for
    // its lifts — each proven in a fork of the judgment (`teach_lifts`) — and
    // its head written without them. A head renders on its own line, so the
    // clause patches above and the rosters below read the lines they judged.
    let lifted = project_decls() |> fold(0, apply_lift)
    // The catch-alls over sums last: each is written at its arm's pattern
    // node and the module rendered whole, which moves lines, so every patch
    // that reads a line number has run by now; a module those patches
    // changed is refused here and rewritten by the next run's judgment.
    let rostered = apply_pattern_tightenings(rosters)
    if len(cands) + len(rosters) == 0 && released == 0 && lifted == 0 {
      print_string("mentl tighten: nothing to tighten — no declared row is an inventory or over-declared, no `own` is refuted, no head writes a type the judgment derives, and no catch-all stands over a sum\n")
      0
    } else {
      print_string("tighten: {int_to_str(n)} of {int_to_str(len(cands))} clause(s) applied, {int_to_str(released)} ownership marker(s) released, {int_to_str(lifted)} head(s) lifted of the types the judgment derives, {int_to_str(rostered)} of {int_to_str(len(rosters))} catch-all arm(s) written as the roster each stood for — the medium authored these; verify with a fresh check + the gates\n")
      if n + released + lifted + rostered > 0 { 0 } else { 1 }
    }
  } ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default ~> infer_context
}

// THE WRITTEN TYPES THE JUDGMENT DERIVES, LIFTED. The function declarations
// of the entry's own layer, read off the decls column so a nested declaration
// is among them; each head with lifts is written without them, the file read
// again for each so one file's heads patch in turn, and the lifts said as the
// gradient says them.
fn project_decls() = decl_handles() |> filter(is_fn_decl) |> filter(in_project_layer)

fn is_fn_decl(h) = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NStmt(FnStmt(_, _, _, _, _)) => true,
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
  | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => false,
}

fn in_project_layer(h) = match diag_scope_of() {
  ScopeProject(layer) => module_layer_of_handle(graph_module_of(h)) == layer,
  ScopeAll | ScopeNone => true,
}

fn apply_lift(acc, h) = match teach_lifts(h) {
  [] => acc,
  lifts => match lifted_module_text(h, lifts) {
    Some((module, text)) => {
      module_write(module, text)
      print_string("tighten: {body_decl_name(graph_node_body(h))} ({module}:{int_to_str(decl_start_line(h))})  {show_teaching(TeachLift(lifts))}\n")
      acc + 1
    },
    None => acc,
  },
}

// THE CATCH-ALLS OVER SUMS, WRITTEN AS THEIR ROSTERS (Morgan, 2026-10-13: a
// `_` over a sum is the roster the author declined to write). A site is a
// refusal the judgment banked (`catch_all_refusals`, src/query.mn): the
// catch-all arm's pattern node and the roster it stands for, so the
// rewrite is ONE WRITE AT THE NODE — the roster registered over the
// catch-all arm — and the page is its projection: the module rendered whole
// from its own module node through the formatter's render, its conservation
// gate and its verdict. No second parse and no coordinate join: the graph
// that judged the match is the graph the roster is written into. A module
// is rewritten only when its file is canonical and unchanged since the
// judgment, because a render that changed anything but the roster would
// write layout the author did not ask for, or undo a clause the passes
// above just patched at a line; each refusal says which.
fn apply_pattern_tightenings(sites) = fold(0, roster_module(sites, ??, ??), link_modules())

// The sites of the entry's own layer: a user's `tighten` never writes the
// library its program links, as its reports never name it (ScopeProject).
fn project_sites(sites) = match diag_scope_of() {
  ScopeProject(layer) => filter({ (pat, _) => module_layer_of_handle(graph_module_of(pat)) == layer }, sites),
  ScopeAll | ScopeNone => sites,
}

// One module of the link with its sites written: the sites the module holds,
// its file, the text its judgment read and its declarations, all off its
// node.
fn roster_module(sites, acc, mh) = match filter({ (pat, _) => graph_module_of(pat) == mh }, sites) {
  [] => acc,
  mine => {
    let m = module_of(mh)
    let verdict = roster_render(m.file, m.text, m.decls, mine) ~> arena
    fmt_print_refused(verdict)
    fmt_write(verdict)
    if fmt_is_render(verdict) {
      print_string("tighten: {m.file} — {int_to_str(len(mine))} catch-all arm(s) written as the roster each stood for\n")
      acc + len(mine)
    } else {
      acc
    }
  },
}

// One module's render with its rosters written: its declarations off its
// node, rendered once to prove the file canonical, then with the rosters in
// place, re-parsed and rendered again (the formatter's own fixpoint), the
// conservation gate stating the `_` each wildcard arm consumed.
fn roster_render(path: String, judged: String, decls, sites) = {
  let src = fs_read_file(path)
  let before = respell(src, format_program(decls))
  if src != judged {
    FmtRefused(path, ["changed since it was judged — run `mentl tighten` again"])
  } else if before != src {
    match first_changed_line(src, before) {
      (n, was, now) => FmtRefused(
        path,
        [
          "not canonical at line {int_to_str(n)} — the roster is written by rendering the module whole, which may change nothing else; `mentl fmt` first",
          "- {was}",
          "+ {now}"
        ]
      ),
    }
  } else {
    let consumed = flat_map(write_roster, sites)
    let first = respell(src, format_program(decls))
    let again = parse_one_module(mint_module(path_to_module(path), path, first, User))
    if parse_refusals() > 0 {
      FmtRefused(path, ["the render does not parse — the formatter's defect, reported above"])
    } else {
      let rendered = respell(first, format_program(again))
      match fmt_losses(src, rendered, consumed) {
        [] => FmtRenders(path, src, rendered),
        lines => FmtRefused(path, lines),
      }
    }
  }
} ~> format_default ~> diag_bank

// One site's write: the roster written at the catch-all arm's own pattern
// node, so the arm, its body and the comment the arm carries stay where they
// are. Answers the atom the roster leaves out of the arm's pattern — a
// wildcard arm's `_`, which the roster says in constructors; a binder
// survives as the name over the grouped alternation.
fn write_roster(refusal) = match refusal {
  (pat, roster) => {
    let consumed = consumed_by(pat)
    graph_register_node(pat, NPat(roster_pattern(roster, graph_span_of(pat))))
    consumed
  },
}

// The roster as the pattern written in the catch-all's place: each
// constructor with its payload left to `_`, Bool's two by their literals,
// their alternation under the arm's binder when it bound one. The module is
// rendered from these nodes and parsed again, so nothing judges them.
fn roster_pattern(roster, span) = match roster {
  Roster(binder, ctors) => match binder {
    Some(name) => PAs(name, npat(roster_alternation(ctors, span), span)),
    None => roster_alternation(ctors, span),
  },
}

fn roster_alternation(ctors, span) = match ctors {
  [one] => roster_ctor(span, one),
  many => PAlt(map({ c => npat(roster_ctor(span, c), span) }, many)),
}

fn roster_ctor(span, entry) = match entry {
  ("True", 0) => PLit(LVBool(true)),
  ("False", 0) => PLit(LVBool(false)),
  (cname, k) => PCon(cname, map({ _ => npat(PWild, span) }, range(0, k))),
}

fn consumed_by(pat) = match pat_of(pat) {
  PWild => ["_"],
  PAs(_, inner) => consumed_by(inner),
  PAlt(ps) => flat_map(consumed_by, ps),
  PVar(_) | PLit(_) | PCon(_, _) | PTuple(_) | PList(_, _) | PRecord(_, _) => [],
}

// The batch applier: locate every candidate (drop unmappable spans),
// order by (path, line DESCENDING) — the negated-line sort key — so each
// file patches bottom-up and earlier offsets never shift, then fold with
// one open file at a time (read on path change, write on leave).
fn apply_tightenings(cands) = {
  let located = flatten(map(
    {
      // The candidate CARRIES its module, so the patch knows which file to
      // open without a containment scan placing a line in a range. The path
      // is the module's own; the span is already the developer's line.
      (declared, used, name, span, dmod) => match span {
        Span(sl, _, _, _) => map((path) => (path, 0 - sl, name, declared, used), module_path_list(dmod)),
      },
    },
    cands
  ))
  tighten_fold(sort(located), 0, "", "", 0)
}

fn tighten_fold(items: [(String, Int, String, EffRow, EffRow)], i, cur_path, cur_text, applied) = if i >= len(items) {
  if byte_len(cur_path) > 0 { fs_write_file(cur_path, cur_text) }
  applied
} else {
  match list_index(items, i) {
    (path, negline, name, declared, used) => {
      let line1 = 0 - negline
      let text0 = if path == cur_path {
        cur_text
      } else {
        if byte_len(cur_path) > 0 { fs_write_file(cur_path, cur_text) }
        fs_read_file(path)
      }
      match patch_clause_residue(text0, line1, declared) {
        Some(t2) => {
          let residue = clause_residue(declared)
          let wrote =
            if byte_len(residue) == 0 { "(no clause — the row is projected)" } else { "with {residue}" }
          print_string("tighten: {name} ({path}:{int_to_str(line1)})  with {show_effrow(declared)}  →  {wrote}\n")
          tighten_fold(items, i + 1, path, t2, applied + 1)
        },
        None => {
          eprint_string("mentl tighten: {name} at {path}:{int_to_str(line1)} — no single-line with-clause; skipped\n")
          tighten_fold(items, i + 1, path, text0, applied)
        },
      }
    },
  }
}

// The RESIDUE of an authored clause — what inference cannot say: its
// negations and its instance pins. Bare positive names are the projected
// row written by hand (an inventory) and leave. Read from the DECLARED row
// the judgment folded (negations in the absent set, pins as parameterized
// presents), never re-parsed from the page; an empty residue means the
// clause itself goes. Everything rendered here is authored source already,
// so it re-parses by construction — the old form wrote the PROVEN row,
// whose type-instances (`Cast(GNode)`) the with-clause grammar cannot
// read back (measured 2026-09-04: twenty E_ConstructorArity from one run).
fn clause_residue(ref declared) = match declared {
  EfRow(p, a, _) => {
    let pins =
      map(show_eff_name, filter({ EParameterized(_, _) => true, ENamed(_) | EPure => false }, p))
    let negs = map({ n => "!{show_eff_name(n)}" }, a)
    show_list(pins ++ negs, { s => s }, " + ")
  },
}

// Rewrite one decl line's with-clause to its residue, or delete the
// clause. The terminator is the first " = " after the clause — the row
// text itself contains no " = ", so the match is the head's binder. None =
// the decl shape is not a single-line with-clause head (never mangle; skip
// and say so).
fn patch_clause_residue(ref text, line1, ref declared) = {
  let ls = line_start_offset(text, line1)
  let le = line_end_offset(text, ls)
  let line = str_slice(text, ls, le)
  let wi = index_of(line, " with ")
  if wi < 0 {
    None
  } else {
    let after = str_slice(line, wi + 6, byte_len(line))
    // The binder is " = " inside the line, or " =" ending it (a body that
    // starts on the next line) — 157 of the wheel's clauses had the second
    // shape and the first sweep skipped every one of them.
    let eq_in = index_of(after, " = ")
    let eq = if eq_in >= 0 { eq_in } else if ends_with(after, " =") { byte_len(after) - 2 } else { -1 }
    if eq < 0 {
      None
    } else {
      let residue = clause_residue(declared)
      let clause = if byte_len(residue) == 0 { "" } else { " with {residue}" }
      let new_line = "{str_slice(line, 0, wi)}{clause}{str_slice(after, eq, byte_len(after))}"
      Some("{str_slice(text, 0, ls)}{new_line}{str_slice(text, le, byte_len(text))}")
    }
  }
}

// One refuted marker released: the head line of the declaration loses the
// `own` before the parameter the judgment named, and the parameter keeps the
// grade its uses infer. A head that does not carry the marker on its first
// line (a parameter list broken over lines) is skipped and named, never
// mangled.
fn apply_own_release(applied, release) = match release {
  (name, span, dmod) => match span {
    Span(sl, _, _, _) => match driver_module_path(dmod) {
      None => {
        eprint_string("mentl tighten: `{dmod}` resolves to no file — `own {name}` skipped\n")
        applied
      },
      Some(path) => match release_own_marker(fs_read_file(path), sl, name) {
        Some(t2) => {
          fs_write_file(path, t2)
          print_string("tighten: `own {name}` ({path}:{int_to_str(sl)})  →  `{name}` — no path consumes it\n")
          applied + 1
        },
        None => {
          eprint_string("mentl tighten: `own {name}` at {path}:{int_to_str(sl)} — not on the head's first line; skipped\n")
          applied
        },
      },
    },
  },
}

fn release_own_marker(text, line1, name: String) = {
  let ls = line_start_offset(text, line1)
  let le = line_end_offset(text, ls)
  let line = str_slice(text, ls, le)
  let marker = "own {name}"
  let at = index_of(line, marker)
  let after = at + byte_len(marker)
  if at < 0 || after < byte_len(line) && is_alnum(byte_at(line, after)) {
    None
  } else {
    let new_line = "{str_slice(line, 0, at)}{str_slice(line, at + 4, byte_len(line))}"
    Some("{str_slice(text, 0, ls)}{new_line}{str_slice(text, le, byte_len(text))}")
  }
}

fn line_start_offset(ref text, line1) = line_start_walk(text, 1, 0, line1)

fn line_start_walk(ref text, at_line, off, line1) = if at_line >= line1 {
  off
} else {
  let n = byte_len(text)
  if off >= n {
    off
  } else if byte_at(text, off) == 10 {
    line_start_walk(text, at_line + 1, off + 1, line1)
  } else {
    line_start_walk(text, at_line, off + 1, line1)
  }
}

fn line_end_offset(ref text, ls) = {
  let n = byte_len(text)
  if ls >= n { n } else if byte_at(text, ls) == 10 { ls } else { line_end_offset(text, ls + 1) }
}

// ─── serve_run — LSP transport on stdio
//
// vim / helix / VSCode (with Mentl extension) consume this. The
// kernel is identical to edit_run; the surface adapter (lsp_adapter)
// is OUTERMOST. Per the LSP-adapter walkthrough substrate.
fn serve_run() = {
  // The transport reads a LIVE graph: handle_did_open judges the opened
  // module, and the judgment's writes land in infer_context installed HERE,
  // at the session boundary, so the graph persists across every hover,
  // definition and reference the session answers.
  lsp_session() ~> wasi_filesystem ~> infer_context ~> progress_stderr(false)
  0
}

// ─── doc_run — documentation projection (F.1 substrate)
//
// Per the doc-handler walkthrough: doc_handler reads the same graph the compile
// handler proves on; render handlers (md / html / llms / terminal)
// project per --target flag; transport handlers emit per stdout /
// file_io / http_serve.
//
// Named follow-up: Hβ.f1.doc-handler-substrate (the lib/doc/ chunks
// per F.1 §13 file-creation order). Today this routes through
// mentl_default + diagnostics for the projection-only mode; the
// dedicated doc_handler is gated.
fn doc_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  // The link the check reads (`driver_link`): a module judged alone misses
  // the vocabulary its layer supplies, and `mentl doc` opened with
  // E_MissingVariable noise on a clean program.
  {
    driver_link(entry_module)
    doc_render(entry_module, module_cells())
  } ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default ~> infer_context
  0
}

// The read path by part (src/sections.mn): the map, or the part a name
// answers, on stdout.
fn map_run(name) = match read_path_map(name) ~> wasi_filesystem {
  (text, code) => {
    print_string(text)
    code
  },
}

// The documentation projection: the entry module's declarations, each with
// its judged type and the first line of its attached prose — the weave's
// comment cells read back, the same edge `mentl <file:line>` speaks as
// `Lede:` and the Why engine walks. Nothing here is authored twice: the
// type is the judgment's, the prose is the graph's.
fn doc_render(entry_module: String, cells) = each(doc_render_cell(entry_module), cells)

fn doc_render_cell(entry_module: String, cell) = match cell {
  (_h, path, _, decls) => if path == entry_module { each(doc_render_decl, decls) } else { () },
}

// Each declaration as the one head projection renders it: a function its
// head (`name(p: T) -> R with row`), a module value its type, a type its
// constructors and an effect its ops — the lines `mentl where` gives them —
// each followed by its lede.
fn doc_render_decl(h) = {
  let body = graph_node_body(h)
  match body {
    NStmt(FnStmt(name, _, _, _, _)) => doc_render_lines([doc_value_line(name)], h),
    NStmt(LetStmt(_, _, _)) => match body_decl_name(body) {
      "" => (),
      name => doc_render_lines([doc_value_line(name)], h),
    },
    NStmt(HandlerDeclStmt(name, _, _, _, _, _)) => doc_render_lines([doc_value_line(name)], h),
    NStmt(TypeDefStmt(_, _)) => doc_render_lines(type_level_lines(h), h),
    NStmt(EffectDeclStmt(_, _, _)) => doc_render_lines(type_level_lines(h), h),
    NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NModule(_) | NLink(_) | NTypeAnn(_) | NName(_)
    | NRow(_) | NParam(_) => (),
  }
}

// A value's line: a function's head, anything else `name : T`, its free
// variables named as a developer writes them.
fn doc_value_line(name: String) = match env_lookup(name) {
  Some((sch, _, _)) => match chase_type_deep(judgment_ty(sch)) {
    TFun(params, ret, row) => show_head(name, params, ret, row),
    ty @ (
      TInt | TFloat | TUnit | TByte | TAddr | TList(_) | TTuple(_) | TVar(_) | TName(_, _) | TRecord(_)
      | TRecordOpen(_, _) | TRefined(_, _) | TReprPin(_, _) | TCont(_, _, _, _) | TAlias(_, _) | TValue(_)
    ) => "{name} : {show_type(ty)}",
  },
  None => name,
}

fn doc_render_lines(lines, h) = {
  let prose = graph_comment_at(h)
  let lede = if len(prose) > 0 { "\n    {first_sentence(prose)}" } else { "" }
  print("{join("\n", lines)}{lede}\n")
}

// Rendering this roster through md / html / llms handlers is the named
// peer Hβ.f1.doc-handler-substrate; the terminal form above is the one every
// render handler projects from.
//
// ─── compile_run / run_run — emit WAT / compile + execute
//
// compile_run emits WAT to stdout (CI use case; shell pipeline).
// run_run = compile + a Process handler over the SAME compile graph — a
// handler swap, not a re-invoked toolchain. On WASI no handler can actually
// exec (see process_no_exec); the install shim owns that seam and returns the
// program's real exit code. The process_exec op is
// the ONLY external surface (the wasmtime wire, peer of the browser/nvim
// wires) — its handler body crosses out of the medium into the host
// process boundary, named Hβ.cli.process-exec-wire.
fn compile_run(target) = {
  compile(target) ~> wat_stdout ~> wasi_filesystem ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version())
  // The dispatch exit-code contract: main's value IS the process exit.
  // Refusal paths already exit nonzero through the gate; success is 0.
  0
}

// `mentl asm [file.wat]` projects the emitter's text to wasm bytes. The file
// is read verbatim; without a file the WAT comes from stdin. A refusal writes
// no partial binary and identifies the offending source position.
fn asm_run(source) = match source {
  Some(path) => if fs_exists(path) {
    asm_write(fs_read_file(path))
  } else {
    eprint_string("mentl asm: no file at {path}\n")
    2
  },
  None => asm_write(read_stdin()),
} ~> wasi_filesystem

fn asm_write(wat) = match assemble(wat) {
  Assembled(bytes) => {
    print_string(bytes)
    0
  },
  AsmRefused(msg, at) => match asm_line_col(wat, at) {
    (line, col) => {
      eprint_string("mentl asm: {msg} at {line}:{col}\n")
      1
    },
  },
}

// `mentl resume <image>` — the compile projection re-entered from a
// persisted image (pipeline.mn resume_image); same outer belt as compile.
fn resume_run(img) = {
  resume_image(img) ~> wat_stdout ~> wasi_filesystem ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version())
  0
}

// Per Hβ.first-light.compile-stdin-substrate: stdin-source compile entry.
// `mentl` with no args reads stdin via WASI fd_read (canonical Unix
// `cmd <input` semantics). compile_stdin (pipeline.mn) carries its own
// handler chain — wat_stdout for emit + wasi_filesystem (stdin via fd 0
// goes through the same handler) + the substrate stack.
fn compile_stdin_run() = {
  compile_stdin() ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version())
  0
}

// ─── run_run — the host runs the module (`mentl run`)
//
// WASI has no exec, and the medium carries no seam past it: a compiled
// module is run by the HOST. The mentl command compiles the program,
// assembles the module and executes it in one step (tools/wt-env.sh, the
// host's one home for that step); the browser's worker instantiates what
// the page compiles. The compiler reached directly with `run` can only
// emit, and says so — it never fabricates a program's exit.
fn run_run(target) = {
  eprint_string("mentl run: the host runs the module — the mentl command compiles {target}, assembles the module and executes it; the compiler reached directly can only emit (mentl compile {target})\n")
  2
}

// The landing pass (`mentl land`) sequences the gates, and each gate is a
// process: the mentl command runs them (tools/state.sh). The compiler
// reached directly with `land` can judge the tree but never land it, and
// says so.
fn land_run() = {
  eprint_string("mentl land: the host runs the landing pass — the mentl command sequences the gates, each a process; the compiler reached directly judges (mentl check, mentl verify) and never lands\n")
  2
}

// ─── check_run — diagnostics; no emit
//
// Same handler chain as compile_run minus wat_stdout — the diagnostic
// pipeline is the value; emission is suppressed.
//
// check's exit code IS the ledger, read live. It reported E_MissingModule and
// exited 0 — a claim about the program ("fine") that the handler one frame away
// knew was false. The read must sit INSIDE the chain: diagnostics_handler is
// installed here, so the op resolves to the same install that did the counting.
// Safe by measurement: nothing in tools/ or .githooks/ reads check's exit code
// (every gate drives the wheel through stdin), so this cannot regress a gate —
// and on the wheel's own source it will now exit nonzero, which is simply true
// and is exactly what the census ratchet is driving to zero.
// pipeline_check hands back its own error count — it owns the handler that did
// the counting. Reading the tally from a handler installed out here returned 0
// forever, because innermost-wins means those reports never reached this
// install: the counter and the count are one handler. That cost a build to find.
//
// The chain this fn used to install is gone with it: pipeline_check already
// installs wasi_filesystem / graph_handler / diagnostics_handler
// at its own foot, so those were shadowed duplicates, and the surviving
// expression is pure arithmetic over the count.
//
// No target is the PROJECT, whose entry is `main` wherever the resolver finds
// it — the same default the board and the session open on.
fn check_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  if pipeline_check(entry_module) > 0 { 1 } else { 0 }
}

// ─── at_run — the cursor-address transport (`mentl <file>:L[:C]`)
//
// The smallest transport of the one read: resolve the address to a graph
// node ONCE (the three-case line rule over the span index every node
// writes at birth), project the eight aspects THROUGH the same CursorView
// `mentl edit` reads (cursor_at_handle — by handle, no re-resolution),
// and render the facets whose content is non-trivial (the voice's
// silence predicate at the terminal). Spans render back to file:line against
// the text the module's node carries — the text its judgment read.
fn at_run(target, line, col) =
  address_project(driver_link(target), target, line, col) ~> patch_to_file ~> terminal_transport ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> infer_context

// accept_run — the second graph operation at the address surface (C4).
// `mentl <address>` PROJECTS the position; `mentl accept <address>` DRAWS
// the accept edge there: the same resolution the address form runs, the
// same eight-aspect view, and then the one proven survivor is accepted
// through the transport's own accept path (accept_at — the edge first,
// the splice as its projection, the module re-derived under the same
// graph). The projection that follows is the address form's, over the
// re-derived graph, so what the verb prints is the position's new truth
// with the Why walking to the proposal it came from. A tie or a filled
// position draws nothing, says why on stderr, and exits 1.
fn accept_run(target, line, col) =
  accept_project(driver_link(target), target, line, col) ~> patch_written ~> terminal_transport ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> infer_context

// The rename verb: decided by the rename module (`rename_at`), and its files
// written only when the renamed program keeps every edge and judges clean.
fn rename_run(target, line, col, name) =
  rename_settle(rename_at(target, line, col, name)) ~> format_default ~> wasi_filesystem ~> infer_context

fn rename_settle(v) = match v {
  Renamed(n, files) => {
    each(rename_file_written, files)
    print_string("rename: {int_to_str(n)} name(s) written in {int_to_str(len(files))} file(s); the program draws every edge it drew\n")
    0
  },
  RenameRefused(_, why) => {
    print_string("rename: nothing written — {why}\n")
    1
  },
}

fn rename_file_written(f) = match f {
  (path, src, text) => {
    fmt_write(FmtRenders(path, src, text))
    print_string("rename: wrote {path}\n")
  },
}

// The draw writes the module's new text and nothing else; the projection is
// over the link derived again from that text, as the session's accept
// projects over the module its re-judgment leaves. The module read before
// the write carries the old text, and slicing the new node's span out of it
// printed `?` where the accepted `1` stands.
fn accept_project(found, target: String, line, col) = if accept_draw(found, target, line, col) == 0 {
  address_project(driver_link(target), target, line, col)
} else {
  1
}

// The accept's DRAW — the position resolved by the address's own rule, the
// edge drawn and the text written through whatever PatchWrite is installed —
// shared by the CLI verb and the resident session, which differ only in what
// re-judges the write. Answers the exit code the verb's verdict is.
fn accept_draw(found, target: String, line, col) = if line <= 0 {
  eprint_string("mentl accept: an accept names one position — {target}:<line>:<col>\n")
  1
} else {
  match address_node(found, target, line, col) {
    None => 1,
    Some(ph) => {
      patch_set_target(target)
      if accept_at(ph, cursor_at_handle(ph)) { 0 } else { 1 }
    },
  }
}

// session_answer — the resident session's verbs, beside the cold dispatch
// they answer for (dispatch_invocation, above), and handed to session_run
// as a value because the transports live in mcp.mn and the verbs live here
// (imports flow main → mcp, never back). The address (its eight aspects and
// the line-0 field), the accept, query, audit and teach run the SAME
// projection halves the cold verbs run, over the graph the session already
// holds. A process-level verb answers MISS and re-runs cold rather than
// answering about the wrong graph; an address names any module the link
// carries (its spans are its own coordinates), and a module the link does
// not carry refuses as the cold verb does, which is MISS as well. The
// whole-link verbs — query, audit, teach — read the ENTRY's graph, so a
// target other than the entry is the cold verb's to answer.
//
// THE ACCEPT ANSWERS HERE because its edge is the session's: drawn into the
// resident graph, the provenance survives into every read the session
// answers after it, where the cold route drew it in a process that ended
// with the reply — the next projection at that position said "int literal".
//
// An answer hands back the manifest the session LEAVES, because a writer
// moves the tree, and the session that answered it has already judged the
// move.
fn session_answer(line, manifest) = match parse_cli_args(session_argv(line)) {
  // the cold route says a parse error whole: on stderr, exit 1
  ParseError(_) => (session_miss(), manifest),
  Invocation(verb) => match verb {
    VAccept(target, l, c) => session_accept(target, l, c, manifest),
    VEdit(_) | VDoc(_) | VMap(_) | VServe | VSpace(_) | VTighten(_) | VFmt(_, _) | VCompile(_)
    | VAsm(_) | VResume(_) | VCompileStdin | VRun(_, _) | VLand | VCheck(_) | VAt(_, _, _)
    | VRename(_, _, _, _) | VTest(_) | VAudit(_) | VQuery(_, _) | VTeach(_) | VRepl | VMarch
    | VVerify(_) | VMcp | VSession | VNew(_) | VWith(_, _) | VWelcome => (session_read(verb), manifest),
  },
}

// The verbs that read the session without moving it. The address and the
// View are one resolution with two projections (src/space.mn): the text the
// terminal reads, and the JSON the page paints.
fn session_read(verb) = match verb {
  VAt(target, l, c) => session_whole(session_address(target, l, c)),
  VSpace(addr) => match addr {
    Some(SpaceAt(target, l, c, needle)) => session_whole(session_banked(() => space_project(session_module(target), target, l, c, needle))),
    None => session_miss(),
  },
  VQuery(target, q) => if target == "main" {
    "{render_query_result(ask(parse_query_string(target, q)))}\n"
  } else {
    session_miss()
  },
  VAudit(target) => if target == "main" { audit_project(link_entry_decls()) } else { session_miss() },
  // A bare `teach` means "teach HERE" — the resident entry is here.
  VTeach(target) => match target {
    Some(m) => if m == "main" { teach_project(link_entry_decls()) } else { session_miss() },
    None => teach_project(link_entry_decls()),
  },
  // The cold-only verbs answer MISS, so they take the cold path.
  VEdit(_) | VDoc(_) | VMap(_) | VServe | VTighten(_) | VFmt(_, _) | VCompile(_) | VAsm(_)
  | VResume(_) | VCompileStdin | VRun(_, _) | VLand | VCheck(_) | VAccept(_, _, _)
  | VRename(_, _, _, _) | VTest(_) | VRepl | VMarch | VVerify(_) | VMcp | VSession | VNew(_)
  | VWith(_, _) | VWelcome => session_miss(),
}

// THE SESSION'S ACCEPT. The edge is drawn and the file written exactly as
// the CLI's accept draws and writes them (accept_draw — the one home), and
// then the session's OWN living check re-judges what the write moved — the
// motion an outside edit makes, through the one door that tracks the tree —
// before the position is projected over the result.
fn session_accept(target: String, l, c, manifest) = match session_banked(() => accept_draw(session_module(target), target, l, c)) {
  (drawn, _) => if drawn != 0 {
    (session_miss(), manifest)
  } else {
    let moved = session_current(manifest)
    (session_whole(session_address(target, l, c)), moved)
  },
}

// The module a person named at the session: the name resolved as the cold
// verb resolves the one it is handed, then the module of the session's link
// read from that file — so the session and the cold verb address one module,
// and a module the link does not hold answers nothing.
fn session_module(target: String) = match driver_module_path(target) {
  Some(file) => find({ mh => module_of(mh).file == file }, link_modules()),
  None => None,
}

// AN ANSWER IS RESIDENT ONLY WHEN IT IS WHOLE. A verb that refuses says why
// on stderr and gives its verdict as the exit code, and the session's wire
// carries neither: the socket's stderr is the SERVER's terminal and a wire
// answer has no exit code. Measured 2026-10-02 on boot 713745c6 with a
// session running: `mentl main.mn:999:1` answered nothing at exit 0, where
// the cold verb prints "main has 4 lines; 999 is past the end" and exits 1.
// A refusal answers MISS, and the cold route says it whole.
fn session_whole(result) = match result {
  (exit, lines) => if exit == 0 { str_concat_all(lines) } else { session_miss() },
}

// One address projection with its lines banked: the core the CLI serves
// (address_project, accept_draw) runs under mcp's collecting console, so the
// lines return to the caller instead of landing on fd 1, beside the
// projection's own exit code. A write lands through patch_written, which
// re-derives nothing: the session re-judges what moved.
fn session_banked(project) = {
  let exit = project()
  (exit, console_taken())
} ~> console_bank ~> patch_written ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default

// The address projection, banked — the one bracket the session's reads, its
// accept's projection and the mcp gate's at tool all answer through.
fn session_address(target: String, l, c) =
  session_banked(() => address_project(session_module(target), target, l, c))

// session_at_read — the mcp gate's at tool, the same banked core: passed
// INTO mcp_run as a value (a stored fn carrying its row — the
// transport-as-value the effect-poly ctor landing made a capability).
fn session_at_read(line, col) = match session_address("main", line, col) {
  (_, lines) => lines,
}

// ─── test_run — Test effect handler chain
//
// Per EH §1.3 + lib/test.mn: assert / assert_eq / assert_near
// dispatch through assert_reporter (runtime) + verify_assert
// (compile-time lifting). Named follow-up: Hβ.test.lib-test-mn-substrate.
//
// test = the Test effect handler chain over the checked graph. The test
// module is checked (its assertions typecheck against the one graph), then
// its check arms dispatch through assert_reporter — assert/assert_eq are
// the Test effect's ops, the handler decides pass/fail (lib/test.mn: "the
// handler decides what happens"). The reporter is a handler swap: the same
// test source runs under assert_reporter (pass/fail counts) or a benchmark
// handler (timing). Execution of the test fns reaches the process boundary
// (Hβ.cli.process-exec-wire, shared with run); the check + reporter chain
// is the structural form, real now.
fn test_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    // default test entry module
    None => "test",
  }
  // A DIRECTORY target is the BATTERY: every .mn inside is a self-describing
  // gate (its first comment declares `// expect: N` — the expectation lives
  // ON the artifact, SYNTAX §Comments), compiled IN THIS PROCESS with a
  // fresh handler chain per micro (handler state initializes at install —
  // per-micro isolation is the install itself), each WAT written to
  // .build/test/, and a manifest printed for the shim's exec loop (WASI has
  // no process-spawn; the shim owns exec — the one seam). One process
  // replaces one wasmtime invocation per micro.
  // The directory probe and the battery run in the SAME handler extent the
  // single-file sibling installs — the fs probe itself is a filesystem op,
  // so the bracket covers the scrutinee too; test_check_run keeps its own
  // inner chain, which shadows these installs for its extent.
  {
    match fs_list_dir(entry_module) {
      Some(_) => battery_run(entry_module),
      None => test_check_run(path_to_module(entry_module)),
    }
  } ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> diagnostics_handler
}

fn test_check_run(entry_module) =
  pipeline_check(entry_module) ~> assert_reporter ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version())

// ─── The battery: `mentl test <dir>` ────────────────────────────────────
// The micro battery IS the medium reading its own gates: glob the directory,
// read each micro's own `// expect: N` header, compile each as its own link
// in ONE process, write the WATs, print the manifest. The
// refusal law holds batch-shaped: a micro whose ledger shows an error or a
// hole gets a FAILC line and NO WAT (the same read `gated_plan` makes for
// the CLI; here the boundary is the batch, so the verdict is a manifest line
// instead of proc_exit).
// The fixture's own contract, first line — the expectation lives ON the
// artifact (SYNTAX §Comments), and the grammar mirrors the medium's own
// two verdicts: `// expect: N` (the program compiles and its run answers
// N) and `// expect: refuse E_Class` (the gate withholds the artifact and
// the judgment reports the named class as an error, so the battery asserts
// exactly what the medium asserts); `// check: refuse E_Class` is the
// judgment's half alone — the class banked with nothing lowered, so `mentl
// check` refuses where the compile does. This contract
// is the frontier bash's dissolution channel: a refusal leg becomes a
// fixture the medium reads itself. `// unreachable: a b` is a facet's
// contract: the fn declarations of the fixture's own layer that `main`
// cannot reach, in source order, exactly — the deletion instruction the
// unreachable facet gives a person. `// refs of Name: 1 call 4 pattern` is
// the refs facet's: what stands at each reference to the declaration the
// name denotes, counted by role — calls, values, patterns — the roles with
// none unsaid. `// fmt: canonical` is the formatter's: the fixture is its own
// render, byte for byte, through the render the fmt verb writes. `// declared
// L:C at L2:C2` is the reference edge's: the name at L:C reads the
// declaration that stands at L2:C2. `// rename L:C to NEW: N name(s)` is the
// rename verb's, decided as the verb decides it with nothing written: the
// names it writes, or `refuses <word>` for why it writes none. `// at L:C:
// <shape>` is the caret's topology facet at the operator standing at L:C:
// the shape the verb carries there, in the short form `show_shape_want`
// writes (`<| x 2`, `>< 2 Seq`, `|> 2 product`, `~> rack Distort`, `<~ 1
// stage`), so the sentence's voice may change without a fixture moving.
type Expect
  // the exit, and the prefix of the line a stop must say on fd 2 ("" when
  // the contract names none)
  = ExpectRun(Int, String)
  | ExpectRefuse(String)
  | ExpectCheckRefuse(String)
  | ExpectPropose(Int, Int, ProposeWant)
  | ExpectTeach(Int, TeachWant)
  | ExpectUnreachable(String)
  | ExpectRefs(String, String)
  | ExpectDeclared(Int, Int, Int, Int)
  | ExpectRename(Int, Int, String, String)
  | ExpectCanonical
  | ExpectAt(Int, Int, String)

// A teach fixture's contract (D4): what the gradient answers at the
// declaration on line L — one proven annotation whose source is the text,
// a question naming its alternatives in rank order, a need, or nothing. The
// annotation's own source projection is compared, never the rendered line,
// so the voice may change without a fixture moving.
type TeachWant = WantAdd(String) | WantAskOf(String) | WantNeed | WantLift(String) | WantNoTeach

// A proposal fixture's contract (C7): what the medium must answer at the
// hole — one proven survivor whose source is the text, a tie whose
// divergence is the named ARM (row · type · name · value · shape ·
// behavior), or no candidate at all. The arm binds the classification,
// never the sentence, so the voice may change without a fixture moving.
type ProposeWant = WantFill(String) | WantAsk(String) | WantNone

// The micro's own expectation: first line `// expect: N`,
// `// expect: refuse E_Class`, `// check: refuse E_Class`, or `// propose
// L:C: fill <text>` | `// propose L:C: ask <arm>` | `// propose L:C: none`.
// None = not a battery member, said loudly by the battery.
fn battery_expect(src) = {
  let prefix = "// expect: "
  if byte_len(src) >= 17 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 17), "// check: refuse ") {
    Some(ExpectCheckRefuse(battery_expect_word(src, 17, 17)))
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 11 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 11), "// propose ") {
    battery_propose_expect(src)
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 9 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 9), "// teach ") {
    battery_teach_expect(src)
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 16 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 16), "// unreachable: ") {
    Some(ExpectUnreachable(battery_line_rest(src, 16, 16)))
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 11 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 11), "// refs of ") {
    battery_refs_expect(src)
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 6 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 6), "// at ") {
    battery_at_expect(src)
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 12 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 12), "// declared ") {
    battery_declared_expect(src)
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 10 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 10), "// rename ") {
    battery_rename_expect(src)
  } else if byte_len(src) >= 17 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 17), "// fmt: canonical") {
    Some(ExpectCanonical)
  } else if byte_len(src) < 12 {
    None
  } else if !str_eq(str_slice(src, 0, 11), prefix) {
    None
  } else {
    if byte_len(src) >= 18 && str_eq(str_slice(src, 11, 18), "refuse ") {
      Some(ExpectRefuse(battery_expect_word(src, 18, 18)))
    } else {
      Some(ExpectRun(
        battery_expect_digits(src, 11, 0),
        battery_expect_said(src, 11 + byte_len(battery_expect_word(src, 11, 11)))
      ))
    }
  }
}

// The class name: bytes from `start` to the first whitespace/newline.
fn battery_expect_word(src, start, i) = if i >= byte_len(src) {
  str_slice(src, start, i)
} else {
  let b = byte_at(src, i)
  if b == 10 || b == 13 || b == 32 {
    str_slice(src, start, i)
  } else {
    battery_expect_word(src, start, i + 1)
  }
}

// What a run that stops must say, after its exit: the rest of the first line
// past one space, the prefix of the line the stop writes on fd 2 — empty
// where the contract names none.
fn battery_expect_said(src, i) =
  if i < byte_len(src) && byte_at(src, i) == 32 { battery_line_rest(src, i + 1, i + 1) } else { "" }

fn battery_expect_digits(src, i, acc) = if i >= byte_len(src) {
  acc
} else {
  let b = byte_at(src, i)
  if b >= 48 && b <= 57 { battery_expect_digits(src, i + 1, acc * 10 + (b - 48)) } else { acc }
}

// `// propose L:C: <want>` — the address, then the want: `fill` takes the
// rest of the line (a candidate's source may carry spaces), `ask` the rest
// too (the arm, and what a value question names: `ask value 0 or 1`),
// `none` nothing.
fn battery_propose_expect(src) = {
  let line = battery_expect_digits(src, 11, 0)
  let col_start = battery_skip_digits(src, 11) + 1
  let col = battery_expect_digits(src, col_start, 0)
  let word_start = battery_skip_digits(src, col_start) + 2
  let word = battery_expect_word(src, word_start, word_start)
  let rest_start = word_start + byte_len(word) + 1
  if str_eq(word, "fill") {
    Some(ExpectPropose(line, col, WantFill(battery_line_rest(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "ask") {
    Some(ExpectPropose(line, col, WantAsk(battery_line_rest(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "none") {
    Some(ExpectPropose(line, col, WantNone))
  } else {
    None
  }
}

// `// teach L: <want>` — the declaration's line, then the want: `add`
// takes the rest of the line (an annotation's source carries spaces),
// `ask` the alternatives joined by ` or `, `need` and `none` nothing.
fn battery_teach_expect(src) = {
  let line = battery_expect_digits(src, 9, 0)
  let word_start = battery_skip_digits(src, 9) + 2
  let word = battery_expect_word(src, word_start, word_start)
  let rest_start = word_start + byte_len(word) + 1
  if str_eq(word, "add") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantAdd(battery_line_rest(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "ask") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantAskOf(battery_line_rest(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "need") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantNeed))
  } else if str_eq(word, "lift") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantLift(battery_line_rest(src, rest_start, rest_start))))
  } else if str_eq(word, "none") {
    Some(ExpectTeach(line, WantNoTeach))
  } else {
    None
  }
}

fn battery_skip_digits(src, i) = if i >= byte_len(src) {
  i
} else {
  let b = byte_at(src, i)
  if b >= 48 && b <= 57 { battery_skip_digits(src, i + 1) } else { i }
}

// The bytes from `start` to the end of the line.
fn battery_line_rest(src, start, i) = if i >= byte_len(src) {
  str_slice(src, start, i)
} else {
  let b = byte_at(src, i)
  if b == 10 || b == 13 { str_slice(src, start, i) } else { battery_line_rest(src, start, i + 1) }
}

// `// refs of Name: <roles>` — the name to the colon, the roles after it.
fn battery_refs_expect(src) = {
  let colon = index_of(src, ":")
  Some(ExpectRefs(str_slice(src, 11, colon), battery_line_rest(src, colon + 2, colon + 2)))
}

// `// declared L:C at L2:C2` — the reference's address, then its
// declaration's.
fn battery_declared_expect(src) = {
  let line = battery_expect_digits(src, 12, 0)
  let col_start = battery_skip_digits(src, 12) + 1
  let col = battery_expect_digits(src, col_start, 0)
  let at_start = battery_skip_digits(src, col_start) + 4
  let dline = battery_expect_digits(src, at_start, 0)
  let dcol_start = battery_skip_digits(src, at_start) + 1
  Some(ExpectDeclared(line, col, dline, battery_expect_digits(src, dcol_start, 0)))
}

// `// at L:C: <shape>` — the address, then the shape's short form after the
// colon and one space.
fn battery_at_expect(src) = {
  let line = battery_expect_digits(src, 6, 0)
  let col_start = battery_skip_digits(src, 6) + 1
  let col = battery_expect_digits(src, col_start, 0)
  let want_start = battery_skip_digits(src, col_start) + 2
  Some(ExpectAt(line, col, battery_line_rest(src, want_start, want_start)))
}

// `// rename L:C to NEW: <want>` — the address, the name to its colon, the
// verdict after it.
fn battery_rename_expect(src) = {
  let line = battery_expect_digits(src, 10, 0)
  let col_start = battery_skip_digits(src, 10) + 1
  let col = battery_expect_digits(src, col_start, 0)
  let name_start = battery_skip_digits(src, col_start) + 4
  let name_end = name_start + index_of(str_slice(src, name_start, byte_len(src)), ":")
  Some(ExpectRename(
    line,
    col,
    str_slice(src, name_start, name_end),
    battery_line_rest(src, name_end + 2, name_end + 2)
  ))
}

// THE RENAME BATTERY: the rename at the address decided as the verb decides
// it, nothing written, and the contract holds when the verdict is the one the
// fixture states — the names it writes, or the refusal by its word.
fn battery_rename_step(stem, path: String, line, col, name: String, want: String) = {
  let v = rename_at(path, line, col, name) ~> format_default ~> infer_context
  let got = match v {
    Renamed(n, _) => "{int_to_str(n)} name(s)",
    RenameRefused(r, _) => "refuses {refusal_word(r)}",
  }
  if str_eq(got, want) {
    print_string("PASS {stem}: rename {want}\n")
    0
  } else {
    let why = match v {
      Renamed(_, _) => "",
      RenameRefused(_, reason) => " — {reason}",
    }
    print_string("FAIL(rename) {stem}: wanted {want}, got {got}{why}\n")
    1
  }
}

// THE DECLARATION BATTERY: the name at the address follows its reference
// edge, and the contract holds when the declaration it reaches stands where
// the fixture says — a binder declared at its own position. The edge is read
// inside the judgment that holds the graph; only the position crosses out.
fn battery_declared_step(stem, path: String, line, col, dline, dcol) = {
  let got = match address_node(driver_link(path), path, line, col) {
    None => "no node at the address",
    Some(r) => match graph_ref_decl(r) {
      None => "no declaration",
      Some(d) => match graph_span_of(d) {
        Span(l, c, _, _) => "{int_to_str(l)}:{int_to_str(c)}",
      },
    },
  } ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default ~> infer_context
  let want = "{int_to_str(dline)}:{int_to_str(dcol)}"
  if str_eq(got, want) {
    print_string("PASS {stem}: declared at {want}\n")
    0
  } else {
    print_string("FAIL(declared) {stem}: wanted {want}, got {got}\n")
    1
  }
}

// THE REFS BATTERY: the fixture's link judged as the query verb judges it,
// and the references `mentl query <fixture> "refs of Name"` answers in the
// fixture's own module — the node the link names, so a primitive the library
// calls throughout is counted where the fixture writes it — counted by what
// stands at each. The references are read inside the judgment that holds the
// graph; only the counts cross out of it.
fn battery_refs_step(stem, path: String, name: String, want: String) = {
  let got = match driver_link(path) {
    None => "nothing: the fixture did not load",
    Some(fixture) => name_decls(name) |> flat_map(decl_ref_handles) |> filter({ h => graph_module_of(h) == fixture }) |> map(ref_role) |> refs_role_counts,
  } ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default ~> infer_context
  if str_eq(got, want) {
    print_string("PASS {stem}: refs of {name} {want}\n")
    0
  } else {
    print_string("FAIL(refs) {stem}: wanted {want}, got {got}\n")
    1
  }
}

// THE FORMAT BATTERY: the fixture rendered as the fmt verb renders a file,
// and the contract holds when the render is the fixture. A render that would
// change it names the first line it changes.
fn battery_fmt_step(stem, path: String, src: String) = {
  let verdict = fmt_render_file(path, src)
  match verdict {
    FmtCanonical(_, _) => {
      print_string("PASS {stem}: canonical\n")
      0
    },
    FmtRenders(_, _, rendered) => match first_changed_line(src, rendered) {
      (n, was, now) => {
        print_string("FAIL(fmt) {stem}:{int_to_str(n)} renders `{now}` for `{was}`\n")
        1
      },
    },
    FmtRefused(_, lines) => {
      print_string("FAIL(fmt) {stem}: {join("; ", lines)}\n")
      1
    },
    FmtUnparsed(_, n) => {
      print_string("FAIL(fmt) {stem}: its parse recovered from {int_to_str(n)} problem(s)\n")
      1
    },
  }
}

// The roles in one order — calls, values, patterns, annotations — each with
// its count, a role nothing stands in unsaid.
fn refs_role_counts(roles) =
  ["call", "value", "pattern", "annotation", "row", "arm"] |> flat_map(role_count(roles, ??)) |> join(" ", ??)

fn role_count(roles, role: String) = match count({ r => r == role }, roles) {
  0 => [],
  n => ["{int_to_str(n)} {role}"],
}

// THE PROPOSAL BATTERY (C7): the fixture is judged at its hole through the
// same address resolution and the same eight-aspect view the CLI's
// `mentl <file>:<line>:<col>` builds, and the contract is compared to the
// Verdict itself — structurally, never through the rendered line.
fn battery_propose_step(stem, path: String, line, col, want) = battery_view(
  path,
  line,
  col,
  {
    None => {
      print_string("FAIL(propose) {stem}: no node at {path}:{int_to_str(line)}:{int_to_str(col)}\n")
      1
    },
    Some((_, view)) => match view {
      CursorView(_, propose, _, _, _, _, _, _) => match propose {
        Proposal(p) => if verdict_meets(want, p.verdict) {
          print_string("PASS {stem}: propose {show_want(want)}\n")
          0
        } else {
          print_string("FAIL(propose) {stem}: wanted {show_want(want)}, got {verdict_word(p.verdict)}\n")
          1
        },
      },
    },
  }
)

// THE TEACH BATTERY (D4): the fixture is judged at its declaration's line
// through the address route and the eight-aspect view, and the contract is
// compared to the gradient's Teaching itself. The line must hold a
// declaration: a `none` contract at a blank line met the module's own view,
// which teaches nothing, and passed (measured 2026-10-13 on a fixture whose
// contract had drifted onto a blank line as its comment grew).
fn battery_teach_step(stem, path: String, line, want) = battery_view(
  path,
  line,
  0,
  {
    None => {
      print_string("FAIL(teach) {stem}: no node at {path}:{int_to_str(line)}\n")
      1
    },
    Some((decl_name, view)) => if decl_name == "" {
      print_string("FAIL(teach) {stem}: no declaration at {path}:{int_to_str(line)}\n")
      1
    } else {
      match view {
        CursorView(_, _, _, _, _, _, teach, _) => if teach_meets(want, teach) {
          print_string("PASS {stem}: teach {show_teach_want(want)}\n")
          0
        } else {
          print_string("FAIL(teach) {stem}: wanted {show_teach_want(want)}, got {show_teaching(teach)}\n")
          1
        },
      }
    },
  }
)

// THE CARET'S SHAPE AT AN OPERATOR: the topology facet's own-verb frame at
// the address, compared in the short form the contract is written in.
fn battery_at_step(stem, path: String, line, col, want: String) = battery_view(
  path,
  line,
  col,
  {
    None => {
      print_string("FAIL(at) {stem}: no node at {path}:{int_to_str(line)}:{int_to_str(col)}\n")
      1
    },
    Some((_, view)) => match view {
      CursorView(_, _, topology, _, _, _, _, _) => {
        let got = shape_want_of(topology)
        if str_eq(got, want) {
          print_string("PASS {stem}: at {int_to_str(line)}:{int_to_str(col)} {want}\n")
          0
        } else {
          print_string("FAIL(at) {stem}: wanted {want}, got {got}\n")
          1
        }
      },
    },
  }
)

// The shape of the verb at the caret, or `none` where the caret is not on a
// verb: the last frame of the topology is the node's own.
fn shape_want_of(frames) = fold("none", shape_want_step, frames)

fn shape_want_step(acc, frame) = match frame {
  VerbFrame(_, role) => match role {
    VerbSite(shape) => show_shape_want(shape),
    ChainSource | ChainStage(_, _) | FanBranch(_, _) | ShareBranch(_, _) | FanInput | InstallBody
    | InstallHandler | Recurrence | FeedbackDelay => acc,
  },
}

// The shape's short form: the verb, then its facts in order.
fn show_shape_want(shape) = match shape {
  ShareShape(input, n) => {
    let what = match input {
      Some(name) => name,
      None => "_",
    }
    "<| {what} {int_to_str(n)}"
  },
  FanShape(n, s) => ">< {int_to_str(n)} {schedule_word(s)}",
  ChainShape(k, n, merges) => "|> {int_to_str(k)} {int_to_str(n)}{if merges { " product" } else { "" }}",
  InstallShape(name, effs) => if len(effs) == 0 { "~> {name}" } else { "~> {name} {join(" ", effs)}" },
  LoopShape(depth, owner) => match depth {
    Some(n) => "<~ {int_to_str(n)} {owner}",
    None => "<~ _ {owner}",
  },
}

// THE REACH BATTERY: the fixture's link judged as the query verb judges it,
// and the names `mentl query <fixture> unreachable` lists compared to the
// contract's, in source order. The rows are read inside the judgment that
// holds the graph; only the names cross out of it.
fn battery_reach_step(stem, path: String, want: String) = {
  let dead = {
    driver_link(path)
    reach_report(module_cells()) |> filter(ur_row_is_dead) |> map({ (_, n, _, _) => n }) |> join(" ", ??)
  } ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default ~> infer_context
  if str_eq(dead, want) {
    print_string("PASS {stem}: unreachable {want}\n")
    0
  } else {
    print_string("FAIL(dead) {stem}: wanted {want}, got {dead}\n")
    1
  }
}

// The eight-aspect view at a fixture's address — the one read both
// contracts judge, built as the CLI's `mentl <file>:<line>:<col>` builds it
// — beside the name the node declares (empty where the node is no
// declaration), handed to the contract's judge INSIDE the judgment that
// holds the graph. A node is its handle, and outside the judgment the handle
// names nothing: a verdict's survivor rendered after this chain closed read
// a body the graph did not hold, and every fill compared as `??` (measured
// 2026-10-13 on the battery's eleven fill fixtures, the day the renderer
// stopped reading the record). The judge's answer is the step's exit.
fn battery_view(path: String, line, col, judge) = judge(match address_node(driver_link(path), path, line, col) {
  None => None,
  Some(ph) => Some((body_decl_name(graph_node_body(ph)), cursor_at_handle(ph))),
}) ~> cursor_default ~> synth_default ~> mentl_default ~> infer_context

fn teach_meets(want, teach) = match want {
  WantAdd(text) => match teach {
    TeachAdd(s) => str_eq(suggestion_text(s), text),
    TeachAsk(_) | TeachNeed(_, _, _) | TeachLift(_) | TeachNone => false,
  },
  WantAskOf(texts) => match teach {
    TeachAsk(ss) => str_eq(join(" or ", map(suggestion_text, ss)), texts),
    TeachAdd(_) | TeachNeed(_, _, _) | TeachLift(_) | TeachNone => false,
  },
  WantNeed => match teach {
    TeachNeed(_, _, _) => true,
    TeachAdd(_) | TeachAsk(_) | TeachLift(_) | TeachNone => false,
  },
  WantLift(texts) => match teach {
    TeachLift(ls) => str_eq(join(", ", map(lift_text, ls)), texts),
    TeachAdd(_) | TeachAsk(_) | TeachNeed(_, _, _) | TeachNone => false,
  },
  WantNoTeach => match teach {
    TeachNone => true,
    TeachAdd(_) | TeachAsk(_) | TeachNeed(_, _, _) | TeachLift(_) => false,
  },
}

fn suggestion_text(s) = match s {
  AnnotationSuggestion(ann, _, _) => show_annotation(ann),
}

fn show_teach_want(want) = match want {
  WantAdd(text) => "add {text}",
  WantAskOf(texts) => "ask {texts}",
  WantNeed => "need",
  WantLift(texts) => "lift {texts}",
  WantNoTeach => "none",
}

fn verdict_meets(want, verdict) = match want {
  WantNone => match verdict {
    VNone => true,
    VFill(_, _) => false,
    VAsk(_, _) => false,
  },
  WantFill(text) => match verdict {
    VFill(node, _) => str_eq(render_candidate_source(node), text),
    VNone => false,
    VAsk(_, _) => false,
  },
  WantAsk(arm) => match verdict {
    VAsk(d, _) => str_eq(divergence_arm(d), arm),
    VNone => false,
    VFill(_, _) => false,
  },
}

fn verdict_word(verdict) = match verdict {
  VNone => "none",
  VFill(node, _) => "fill {render_candidate_source(node)}",
  VAsk(d, survivors) => "ask {divergence_arm(d)} ({int_to_str(len(survivors))} survivors)",
}

fn show_want(want) = match want {
  WantNone => "none",
  WantFill(text) => "fill {text}",
  WantAsk(arm) => "ask {arm}",
}

fn battery_wat_path(stem) = ".build/battery/{stem}.wat"

// One micro, one fresh chain — the same chain compile_stdin drives, with
// the emission STREAMED through wat_to_file into the battery's artifact
// directory (the module's text is never held whole — the scope at
// wat_to_string's decl). The HOST runs it: for a run contract the battery
// prints a RUN line naming the module and what it must answer, and the
// command that invoked `mentl test` assembles each one, executes it and
// judges the exit (tools/wt-env.sh, wt_battery) — the compiler reached
// directly can only emit, in the battery exactly as at `mentl run`.
// Returns (what the compile left, error-count, banked classes).
//
// The fixture is a MODULE: its link is its import DAG plus the prelude edge,
// judged as every compile judges one (driver.mn), so a run fixture compiles
// from its module path exactly as a propose fixture is read at one, and the
// program lowered is the one its link holds.
fn battery_compile(module: String, stem) = {
  driver_link_scoped(module, Census)
  let lowered = link_program() |> saturate_pass |> lower_program |> reachable_from_main
  // THE SAME law the CLI compile applies, batch-shaped: an error or a hole
  // refuses, the plan's settle point included. `gated_plan` is the law's one
  // home; this policy counts and continues where the CLI compile proc_exits.
  // The refuse contract reads the classes through the bank, which forwards
  // every report, so every line still reaches stderr.
  let left = gated_write(lowered, battery_wat_path(stem))
  // THE CLASSES, never the rendered lines: the refuse contract compares
  // names, so no substring scan over prose decides a verdict.
  let classes = banked_classes()
  let nerr = diag_errors()
  (left, nerr, classes)
} ~> wasi_filesystem ~> compile_context

fn battery_run(dir) = {
  // The fixture paths are flat before the first micro: each step indexes
  // them, and a flat sequence answers in one step. The fixtures are the
  // directory's own .mn files, read by the one walk (`mn_files`).
  let micros = list_to_flat(mn_files(dir_base(dir), Level))
  // The artifact directory exists before the first module is written; a
  // directory already there answers the same (EEXIST is absorbed). The op,
  // never its impl: the row names what the battery does to the host.
  {
    fs_mkdir(".build")
    fs_mkdir(".build/battery")
  } ~> wasi_filesystem
  battery_loop(micros, 0, 0)
}

// Each micro's compile is an ARENA (lib/arena.mn): what the compile
// allocated is dead once its verdict is printed, except what it stored into
// memory older than the micro, so the battery's peak is one compile's live
// set, never the sum, and a micro's output cannot depend on the micros
// before it unless something it stored outlived it — which the arena's
// census counts as moved.
fn battery_loop(micros: [String], i, fails) = if i >= len(micros) {
  fails
} else {
  let step = battery_step(micros[i]) ~> arena
  battery_loop(micros, i + 1, fails + step)
}

fn battery_step(path: String) = {
  let stem = path_stem(path)
  let src = fs_read_file(path)
  match battery_expect(src) {
    None => {
      print_string("NOEXPECT {stem}\n")
      1
    },
    Some(contract) => match contract {
      // The driver takes the MODULE path — the file without its `.mn`, exactly
      // as the CLI's address form hands it over (path_to_module, cli.mn).
      ExpectPropose(line, col, want) => battery_propose_step(stem, path_to_module(path), line, col, want),
      ExpectTeach(line, want) => battery_teach_step(stem, path_to_module(path), line, want),
      ExpectUnreachable(want) => battery_reach_step(stem, path_to_module(path), want),
      ExpectRefs(name, want) => battery_refs_step(stem, path_to_module(path), name, want),
      ExpectDeclared(line, col, dline, dcol) => battery_declared_step(stem, path_to_module(path), line, col, dline, dcol),
      ExpectAt(line, col, want) => battery_at_step(stem, path_to_module(path), line, col, want),
      ExpectRename(line, col, name, want) => battery_rename_step(stem, path_to_module(path), line, col, name, want),
      ExpectCanonical => battery_fmt_step(stem, path, src),
      ExpectRun(want, said) => match battery_compile(path_to_module(path), stem) {
        (left, nerr, _) => match left {
          ArtifactRefused => {
            print_string("FAILC {stem}\n")
            1
          },
          // The verdict is the host's: the RUN line hands over the module,
          // the exit it must answer, the error count, which the gate has
          // already held at zero, and the line a stop must say; the command
          // that invoked the battery runs the module and prints PASS or
          // FAIL(run) in this line's place.
          ArtifactWritten => {
            print_string("RUN {stem} {battery_wat_path(stem)} {int_to_str(want)} {int_to_str(nerr)} {said}\n")
            0
          },
        },
      },
      ExpectRefuse(cls) => match battery_compile(path_to_module(path), stem) {
        // The contract is the gate's verdict: the artifact withheld, and the
        // named class among what the judgment reported.
        (left, nerr, classes) => if left == ArtifactRefused && any((c) => c == cls, classes) {
          print_string("REFUSE {stem} {cls}\n")
          0
        } else {
          // The verdict names what the judgment DID bank, so a contract that
          // misses says which class stood where the wanted one was expected.
          print_string("FAILR {stem} wanted {cls} (errors={int_to_str(nerr)}; banked: {join(", ", classes)})\n")
          1
        },
      },
      // The judgment's verdict alone: the named class among what it banked
      // with nothing lowered, so `mentl check` refuses where the compile
      // does.
      ExpectCheckRefuse(cls) => match battery_check(path_to_module(path)) {
        (nerr, classes) => if nerr > 0 && any((c) => c == cls, classes) {
          print_string("CHECK {stem} {cls}\n")
          0
        } else {
          print_string("FAILK {stem} wanted {cls} at check (errors={int_to_str(nerr)}; banked: {join(", ", classes)})\n")
          1
        },
      },
    },
  }
}

// The judgment as `mentl check` runs it — the link judged, nothing lowered —
// and what it banked read back as classes beside the error count.
fn battery_check(module: String) = {
  driver_link_scoped(module, Census)
  (diag_errors(), banked_classes())
} ~> wasi_filesystem ~> compile_context

// assert_reporter — the Test effect's pass/fail reporter handler. Each
// check(cond, msg) folds into (passed, failed); get_results projects the
// tally. The handler IS the test framework (no framework needed —
// lib/test.mn). State threads the counts; a failing assertion surfaces its
// message at the WASI boundary.
handler assert_reporter with passed = 0, failed = 0 {
  check(cond, msg) => if cond {
    resume() with passed = passed + 1
  } else {
    eprint_string("  FAIL: {msg}\n")
    resume() with failed = failed + 1
  },
  get_results() => resume((passed, failed)),
}

// ─── audit_run — capability set + severance suggestions
//
// Walks the ~> chain; collects effect rows transitively; emits
// AuditReport. Per DESIGN §9.1 + traces/a-day §1400.
fn audit_run(target) = {
  audit(target) ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> verify_ledger ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version())
  0
}

// `mentl query <module> "<question>"`: a read-only question asked of a
// module's judged graph. A DIRECTORY is a tree of independent programs, each its own
// link as `mentl test <dir>` runs them, and a site facet answers across all
// of them (`query_dir`), found under the roots a module name resolves
// against (`driver_dir_path`). The directory probe is a filesystem op, so
// the bracket covers it, as the battery's does.
fn query_run(target, question) = match driver_dir_path(dir_base(target)) {
  Some(dir) => query_dir(dir, question),
  None => query(target, question),
} ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version())

// Every program in the directory judged on its own fresh chain, each inside
// an arena so the walk's peak is one judgment's live set, and asked the
// question; the sites in each program's own module are the answer. A facet
// that answers anything but sites refuses, naming the ones that do.
fn query_dir(dir: String, question) = {
  let programs = list_to_flat(mn_files(dir, Level))
  let answers = map(query_program(question, ??), programs)
  if any(answer_is_not_sites, answers) {
    eprint_string("mentl query: a directory answers the site facets — refs of NAME, census SHAPE, text NEEDLE, prose NEEDLE, decls\n")
    1
  } else {
    let found = flat_map(answer_sites, answers)
    let unread = len(filter(answer_is_unread, answers))
    if unread > 0 {
      eprint_string("mentl query: {int_to_str(unread)} program(s) in {dir} never joined the weave\n")
    } else {
      ()
    }
    println("→ {int_to_str(len(found))} {fold("site(s)", answer_label, answers)} across {int_to_str(len(programs))} program(s){render_noted_sites(found)}")
    0
  }
}

fn query_program(question, path: String) = query_sites(path_to_module(path), question) ~> arena

fn dir_base(dir: String) =
  if ends_with(dir, "/") { str_slice(dir, 0, byte_len(dir) - 1) } else { dir }

fn answer_is_not_sites(answer) = match answer {
  Some(found) => match found {
    Some(_) => false,
    None => true,
  },
  None => false,
}

fn answer_is_unread(answer) = match answer {
  Some(_) => false,
  None => true,
}

fn answer_sites(answer) = match answer {
  Some(found) => match found {
    Some(pair) => match pair {
      (_, sites) => sites,
    },
    None => [],
  },
  None => [],
}

fn answer_label(label, answer) = match answer {
  Some(found) => match found {
    Some(pair) => match pair {
      (l, _) => l,
    },
    None => label,
  },
  None => label,
}

// ─── verify_run — the medium judges its own source (`mentl verify`)
//
// Reads every bound in `board_bounds()` against the count `census_sites`
// computes live, on ONE judged graph, and refuses when any is breached.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT THAT MADE THIS A LANDING AND NOT A PORT: the shell gate
// answered these same twelve counts with twelve separate invocations of the
// medium — `query src/main.mn "census eta"`, then `"census
// effectful-lambda"`, then nine drift shapes, then the open receiver — each
// one a whole wheel compile thrown away after a regex read one integer out
// of its rendering. Twelve judgments of one program to ask twelve questions
// of one graph. The verb judges once and reads twelve columns off it, which
// is the Carried-Truth Law at the gate layer: the graph already knew.
//
// The board REFUSES rather than reports. A breached bound exits nonzero
// because a gate that narrates is a gate that stops being run (§11 tripwire
// 4), and the one thing this verb may never do is let a rise through with a
// note about it.
// THE WEAVE JUDGMENT, not `pipeline_check`, and this verb learned it the way
// `mentl query` did — by measuring twelve confident zeros on its first run.
// `pipeline_check` judges without leaving the weave the census walks, so
// every shape counted nothing and the board reported twelve bounds holding
// on a graph that held no source. That is `Hβ.query.unreadable-source-
// refusal` reproduced at a second surface, and the fix is its own ruling: a
// board over an unread weave REFUSES the question rather than answering it,
// because the one failure a gate may never have is a green that means
// "nothing was measured" (§11 tripwire 4 — a gate that stops being reported
// stops being run; a gate that measures nothing has stopped being run while
// still being reported).
fn verify_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  // SCOPE ALL, because this is the BOARD — the medium's standing bounds on
  // its own source, not a user reading one file. Every other entry narrows to
  // the module the user named so a compile never buries them in prelude
  // narration; the board is the one reader for which that narrowing is the
  // defect, and while it inherited the narrowing the comment-reference count
  // could only ever see the entry module's prose (measured 2026-09-20:
  // 0 on the entry link against 58 across the modules).
  match driver_link_scoped(entry_module, Census) {
    None => {
      eprint_string("mentl verify: no source to judge — {entry_module} did not load\n")
      1
    },
    // AND THE LINK MUST HAVE JUDGED CLEAN, which is the same vacuity one
    // layer in and the instrument gate is what found it: a module whose own
    // file loads but whose IMPORT does not still names its link, so the arm
    // above admits it, the census walks a graph missing that module's
    // content, and every bound reads a number too small to breach. It
    // answered "12 bound(s) hold" for a program the driver had just refused.
    // A partial measurement is worse than none because it looks like one, so
    // the board reads the judgment's own error count and declines to measure
    // rather than measuring less.
    Some(_) => if diag_errors() > 0 {
      eprint_string("mentl verify: {entry_module} did not judge clean — a board over a refused link measures a program nobody has\n")
      1
    } else {
      verify_board(entry_module)
    },
  }
} ~> stdout_console ~> wasi_filesystem ~> mentl_default ~> infer_context

// THE PROJECT LINE beside the bounds: every narration the judgment made over
// every module of the link, counted once whether or not a register rendered
// it. The wheel carries zero — a warning anywhere in the link refuses, so the
// eight refuted `own` markers the entry-scoped check hid can never return
// unseen.
fn verify_board(entry_module: String) = {
  let board = board_read() ~> query_context
  let narrated = diag_narrations()
  print_string("verify: the medium's verdict on {entry_module}\n")
  each(print_board_line, board)
  let narration_breach = if narrated > 0 {
    print_string("  ✗ {int_to_str(narrated)} narration(s) over every module of the link — ROSE past 0 (they print above, each at its module)\n")
    1
  } else {
    print_string("  · 0 narration(s) over every module of the link — within 0\n")
    0
  }
  let breached = len(filter(board_breached, board)) + narration_breach
  if breached > 0 {
    print_string("verify: {int_to_str(breached)} bound(s) breached — a count the medium keeps about itself rose.\n")
    1
  } else {
    print_string("verify: {int_to_str(len(board))} bound(s) hold.\n")
    0
  }
}

fn print_board_line(entry) = print_string(board_line(entry))

// ─── with_run — the universal escape hatch (env_lookup resolution)
//
// `mentl --with <handler> [path]` installs an arbitrary user-defined
// entry-handler resolved by env_lookup — the SAME mechanism every
// built-in verb uses (the env IS the dispatch table; the type system IS
// the package manager, §5). The handler name resolves against the
// checked program's env; a built-in and a user verb are one lookup.
// Resolution of the handle into the install site is the named peer
// Hβ.cli.user-defined-entry-handler-resolution — the env overlay it
// reads is the per-module env the driver populates (driver IC.3 chase).
fn with_run(handler_name, target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  pipeline_check(entry_module)
  match env_lookup(handler_name) {
    None => eprint_string("mentl --with: no handler named {handler_name} in the checked env\n"),
    Some(_) => eprint_string("mentl --with {handler_name}: resolved; install via Hβ.cli.user-defined-entry-handler-resolution\n"),
  }
  // The verb is named but its install path is unbuilt — the CLI refusal
  // law: a named verb that cannot do its work exits nonzero.
  1
}

// ─── teach_run — terminal voice surface (default)
//
// Bare `mentl` defaults here per EH. Gradient narration; one
// suggestion per turn; Mentl's voice in the terminal for
// vim/helix/terminal-only users.
fn teach_run(target_opt) = {
  let entry_module = match target_opt {
    Some(t) => t,
    None => "main",
  }
  // teach reaches inference — the core installs as the one bracket (this
  // chain was the eighth found missing the analysis handlers, by design
  // pass not by trap; query_default's env_lookup / env_snapshot resolve
  // into the bracket's env).
  teach(entry_module) ~> stdout_console ~> query_context ~> wasi_filesystem ~> infer_context
  0
}

// ─── repl_run — the line-eval cursor
//
// The REPL IS the cursor reading one line at a time: each line is a
// frontend |> infer projection, the result spoken through the voice
// surface (the same teach projection, scoped to one entry). It is the
// `<~` edit loop (cursor_transport) with the line as the caret event —
// one cursor, the terminal transport, fed line-by-line. The stdin line
// reader is the host boundary (Hβ.repl.line-read-wire, peer of the
// process/web/nvim wires); the eval-and-project chain is real now.
fn repl_run() = {
  // The line-eval runs infer; the core installs as the one bracket (the
  // driverless-zero-state class the bounds trap unmasked lives in its law).
  repl_eval_line(read_stdin_line()) ~> stdout_console ~> query_context ~> wasi_filesystem ~> infer_context ~> progress_stderr(false)
  0
}

// repl_eval_line — one cursor read of a source line: lex |> parse |> infer,
// then project the inferred shape through the voice surface. Empty line is
// the empty projection (the cursor at rest), never a fabricated value.
fn repl_eval_line(line) = if byte_len(line) == 0 {
  ""
} else {
  infer_program_converged(line)
  render_query_result(ask(parse_query_string("", "type main")))
}

// ─── new — the welcome's own WRITE
//
// The welcome PROJECTS a directory (project_directory, the READ); `new`
// draws the first edge into it (the WRITE) and then re-projects. One
// graph, two operations, at the shell — `new` is the second operation,
// never a scaffolding engine.
//
// ONE FILE, never a tree. A project directory exists to hold a manifest,
// and the imports ARE the manifest (PLAN §11's novelty audit); a `.mn`
// file carrying `fn main()` IS an executable (SYNTAX §Top-level program
// structure), so `hello.mn` beside you IS the project — and `mentl run
// hello` reaches it through the resolver's own first arm (driver.mn's
// direct read). `{name}/src/main.mn` was Cargo's shape borrowed one level
// down, and the resolver could not even see it.
//
// The seed is a string the medium CARRIES (new_seed). Reading it from a
// repo file made the CLI depend on an artifact outside itself: from any
// directory but the repo, fs_read_file found nothing, returned "" (its
// own surrender-fallback) and `new` wrote a ZERO-BYTE file while printing
// that it had scaffolded the file from the first lesson — a claim about
// the world that was false.
//
// So `new` states nothing it did not read. It never claims "wrote
// hello.mn"; it re-projects the directory and lets the file speak for
// itself — a write followed by the live read is unfalsifiable, and it is
// the same `<~` the cursor session runs (draw the edge, re-project).
fn new_project(name) = {
  scaffold_project(name) ~> wasi_filesystem ~> diagnostics_handler ~> no_state_walked ~> graph_handler(root_version())
  0
}

// String pins on this and the sibling verb/render fns below are Intent
// Boundaries (spec_mangle's law): a param whose only use is a splice
// generalizes, and the word-floor copy renders it as a pointer numeral.
fn scaffold_project(name: String) = {
  let dest = "{name}.mn"
  if fs_exists(dest) {
    // Refusal teaches: the medium never overwrites what it did not write.
    eprint_string("mentl new {name}: {dest} is already here\n\n")
    eprint_string("the medium never overwrites what it did not write.\n")
    eprint_string("  read it:      mentl edit {name}\n")
    eprint_string("  or name another:  mentl new {name}2\n")
    1
  } else {
    fs_write_file(dest, new_seed())
    project_directory()
    0
  }
}

// The seed the medium carries — the smallest program that RUNS and whose
// own comments are the Reason edges it talks about. Not the curriculum
// (the course, examples/pulse/course, is the curriculum, and it is not on
// a user's disk).
// The `\{who\}` escapes emit literal braces, so the FILE gets a live
// interpolation the user's own compile reads.
fn new_seed() =
  "// this file is the whole project — the imports are the manifest.\n//\n// the comment you are reading is not a remark the lexer discards: it is\n// a Reason edge in the graph. ask the medium about this file:\n//   mentl audit   — what it is allowed to do\n//   mentl edit    — the cursor, and what it proposes\n\nimport io\n\n// `|>` is the sequential verb — the string flows into print_string.\n// the topology is on the page; there is no hidden control flow.\nfn greet(who) = \"hello, \{who\} — the medium is reading you back\\n\" |> print_string\n\nfn main() = \{\n  greet(\"world\")\n  0\n\}\n"

// ─── help + error surface ─────────────────────────────────────────
fn print_error_and_help(msg) = {
  eprint_string("mentl: ")
  eprint_string(msg)
  eprint_string("\n\n")
  print_help()
  // A malformed invocation exits 2 (the usage-error convention the gate
  // scripts already speak) — main's value is the process exit code.
  2
}

// The verb catalog — ONE surface projected from the verb table, so the help a
// user reads can never disagree with the parser that reads them. The bare
// `mentl` line (the directory projection, not a named verb) frames the list;
// every verb line is one spec rendered uniformly.
fn verb_catalog() =
  "the verbs:\n  mentl                          — project this directory (a pipe compiles stdin)\n" ++ (verb_specs() |> map(catalog_line) |> str_concat_all) ++ "\na target is a module name or a file path, from any directory.\n" ++ "the surface: PLAN.md §11 (the production bar) · docs/SYNTAX.md (the language)\n"

// One catalog line per spec: `  mentl <name> <hint>` padded to a column, then
// `— <desc>`.
fn catalog_line(spec) = match spec {
  VerbSpec(name, hint, build, desc) => {
    let usage = if byte_len(hint) > 0 { name ++ " " ++ hint } else { name }
    "  mentl " ++ pad_right(usage, 25) ++ "— " ++ desc ++ "\n"
  },
}

// Right-pad with spaces to width w (the alignment column); a string already
// that wide is itself.
fn pad_right(s, w) = s ++ join("", repeat(w - byte_len(s), " "))

fn print_help() = eprint_string(verb_catalog())

// ─── the welcome — bare `mentl` in a terminal, `mentl help` ────────
//
// Not a manpage: a PROJECTION of where you are (the cursor law at the
// shell). The medium lists the current directory through the Filesystem
// capability, names the .mn sources it sees, and offers each one's next
// keystroke; an empty directory gets the beginning. stdout, exit 0 — help
// asked for is never an error (the ERROR surface, print_error_and_help,
// stays on stderr).
fn welcome_run() = {
  print_string("mentl — the medium between intent and execution\n")
  print_string("write what you mean; the medium carries what it can prove\n\n")
  project_directory()
  print_string(verb_catalog())
  0
} ~> wasi_filesystem

// project_directory — the cursor law at the shell: read where you ARE and
// name each source's next keystroke. The welcome opens with it; `new`
// closes with it (which is why `new` never has to claim what it wrote —
// the projection reads the live directory back). An empty directory gets
// the beginning, so the surface is never a dead end.
fn project_directory() = {
  let sources = filter((n) => ends_with(n, ".mn"), unwrap_or(fs_list_dir("."), []))
  if len(sources) > 0 {
    print_string("in this directory:\n")
    each(
      (n) => {
        let stem = str_slice(n, 0, byte_len(n) - 3)
        print_string("  {n}  →  mentl run {stem} · mentl edit {stem} · mentl check {stem}\n")
      },
      sources
    )
    print_string("\n")
  } else {
    print_string("nothing to read here yet — begin:\n  mentl new hello && mentl run hello\n\n")
  }
}
